WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.500
&gt;&gt; Welcome to the session,
just after lunch.

00:00:07.501 --> 00:00:08.970
I think people would
be trickling in here

00:00:08.971 --> 00:00:12.295
because some of them
got lunch late.

00:00:12.296 --> 00:00:15.450
But hopefully we'll see

00:00:15.451 --> 00:00:18.130
a stream of people
coming in soon.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:21.770
So, this is a session
on free inference and

00:00:21.771 --> 00:00:25.235
instinct training,
breakthroughs, and implications.

00:00:25.236 --> 00:00:28.790
We have a Stellar set
of speakers here today.

00:00:28.791 --> 00:00:31.340
Matthai and I are
chairing the session.

00:00:31.341 --> 00:00:35.330
We're just going
to set the stage,

00:00:35.331 --> 00:00:39.885
introduce the speakers,
and hear from them.

00:00:39.886 --> 00:00:42.900
So, in this session,

00:00:42.901 --> 00:00:45.660
our aim is take
the blinders off and fire

00:00:45.661 --> 00:00:47.055
the imagination of

00:00:47.056 --> 00:00:48.960
the systems and machine
learning community.

00:00:48.961 --> 00:00:50.510
Much of the community has focused

00:00:50.511 --> 00:00:53.000
on speeding up training,

00:00:53.001 --> 00:00:54.745
making it more efficient,

00:00:54.746 --> 00:00:57.330
making inference as
cheap as possible.

00:00:57.331 --> 00:01:00.850
Most of the work has focused
on deep neural networks.

00:01:00.851 --> 00:01:03.075
We want to ask the question,

00:01:03.076 --> 00:01:04.615
if all these problems are solved,

00:01:04.616 --> 00:01:07.440
what should be we
looking at next?

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.865
So, we want to do this at

00:01:10.866 --> 00:01:14.610
various levels, the
algorithmic level,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:18.150
hardware level, what new hardware

00:01:18.151 --> 00:01:20.200
should be we considering,

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:24.520
and frameworks and applications.

00:01:24.521 --> 00:01:25.760
What are the new applications

00:01:25.761 --> 00:01:27.290
that we should be considering?

00:01:27.291 --> 00:01:29.660
Not just the standard image

00:01:29.661 --> 00:01:32.570
classification or
translation applications

00:01:32.571 --> 00:01:33.590
that have been driving

00:01:33.591 --> 00:01:37.000
this field for the past
couple of years.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.880
So, in this context,

00:01:39.881 --> 00:01:42.855
we have four speakers here today.

00:01:42.856 --> 00:01:44.800
If you want to look at

00:01:44.801 --> 00:01:46.410
the universities,
they represent, CMU,

00:01:46.411 --> 00:01:47.625
MIT, Berkeley, and Stanford,

00:01:47.626 --> 00:01:49.345
and MSR too, recently.

00:01:49.346 --> 00:01:55.080
So, five organizations
among four speakers.

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:58.930
More importantly they're
all experts in ML theory,

00:01:58.931 --> 00:02:02.590
in algorithms, systems,
hardware, and applications.

00:02:02.591 --> 00:02:05.375
So, we're very, very

00:02:05.376 --> 00:02:06.710
grateful that you're here today

00:02:06.711 --> 00:02:08.565
to share their thoughts with us.

00:02:08.566 --> 00:02:11.500
I'll let Matthai, introduce
our first speaker,

00:02:11.501 --> 00:02:13.870
to kick us off.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:18.645
&gt;&gt; So, our first speaker
is Michael Jordan.

00:02:18.646 --> 00:02:21.810
Who's the Pehong Chen
Distinguished Professor,

00:02:21.811 --> 00:02:23.150
in the Department of Electrical

00:02:23.151 --> 00:02:24.825
Engineering and Computer Science,

00:02:24.826 --> 00:02:26.505
and the Department of Statistics,

00:02:26.506 --> 00:02:28.565
at the University
of California, at Berkeley.

00:02:28.566 --> 00:02:30.260
Professor Jordan, is one of

00:02:30.261 --> 00:02:32.610
the leading forces in
the machine learning community.

00:02:32.611 --> 00:02:34.670
It's a broad statement but it's

00:02:34.671 --> 00:02:36.650
actually true across a lot many,

00:02:36.651 --> 00:02:38.190
many different areas
of machine learning.

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:40.025
He's known for his pivotal work

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:42.390
in broad set of areas,
bridging, computational,

00:02:42.391 --> 00:02:46.965
statistical, cognitive,
and biological sciences.

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:49.575
In particular, his work has been

00:02:49.576 --> 00:02:51.555
central and understanding

00:02:51.556 --> 00:02:53.480
the statistical foundations
of machine learning,

00:02:53.481 --> 00:02:54.850
in addition to
developing algorithms,

00:02:54.851 --> 00:02:56.370
and lot more downstream work.

00:02:56.371 --> 00:02:58.250
So, without further ado,

00:02:58.251 --> 00:03:00.400
here's Professor Michael Jordan.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:06.520
&gt;&gt; Okay. Hi, did you
get my mic on now?

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:14.200
Okay. So, I gather this is
an systems focused crowd.

00:03:14.201 --> 00:03:16.910
So, I will have that in mind.

00:03:16.911 --> 00:03:19.890
My overall message is going
to be machine learning,

00:03:19.891 --> 00:03:21.395
in great growth mode right now.

00:03:21.396 --> 00:03:22.780
It's not one thing,

00:03:22.781 --> 00:03:23.960
it's a whole bunch of different

00:03:23.961 --> 00:03:25.180
kinds of related challenges.

00:03:25.181 --> 00:03:29.850
So, I think about not trying
to build a single database,

00:03:29.851 --> 00:03:31.010
or Unix, or something.

00:03:31.011 --> 00:03:33.320
I like one tool, that solves
all problems in the field.

00:03:33.321 --> 00:03:35.020
It's really more like tools.

00:03:35.021 --> 00:03:37.345
So, think about someone
who builds houses.

00:03:37.346 --> 00:03:38.940
They have a screwdriver,

00:03:38.941 --> 00:03:39.520
and they have a hammer,

00:03:39.521 --> 00:03:40.570
and they have a saw,
and all that,

00:03:40.571 --> 00:03:42.885
and that hadn't changed
over the centuries,

00:03:42.886 --> 00:03:44.410
and it's not going to change.

00:03:44.411 --> 00:03:45.630
So, there's not be one tool,

00:03:45.631 --> 00:03:47.470
this is a bunch of tools.

00:03:47.471 --> 00:03:50.100
So, I have a lot of respect
for systems people,

00:03:50.101 --> 00:03:51.230
and I really want to continue

00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:53.475
the partnership
which is emerging.

00:03:53.476 --> 00:03:55.605
In fact, when I first
went to Berkeley,

00:03:55.606 --> 00:03:57.580
I was coming in as a
machine learning person,

00:03:57.581 --> 00:03:58.855
this is in 1998.

00:03:58.856 --> 00:04:00.840
They said, well, let
me join the AI group?

00:04:00.841 --> 00:04:02.400
I said, "No, I know
nothing about AI,

00:04:02.401 --> 00:04:03.105
and I don't really care,

00:04:03.106 --> 00:04:04.640
I don't want to build
artificial humans.

00:04:04.641 --> 00:04:05.430
That's not interesting to me.

00:04:05.431 --> 00:04:07.300
What I want to partner with
is the systems people,

00:04:07.301 --> 00:04:08.850
because they've got
a perspective that

00:04:08.851 --> 00:04:11.410
I really respect and I want
to learn more about it,

00:04:11.411 --> 00:04:13.570
and I think, there is where
the power is going to be."

00:04:13.571 --> 00:04:16.240
They pretended to want
a label me AI over the years.

00:04:16.241 --> 00:04:18.350
I've never worked on
vision robotics and again,

00:04:18.351 --> 00:04:20.760
I don't really care, I
shouldn't say this on a video.

00:04:20.761 --> 00:04:22.610
But I really care about

00:04:22.611 --> 00:04:25.235
the engineering systems that
really changed the world,

00:04:25.236 --> 00:04:27.840
really large-scale, move
data around, and all that.

00:04:27.841 --> 00:04:29.545
If it has vision or movement,

00:04:29.546 --> 00:04:32.245
that's fine, but it's not
my main focus. All right.

00:04:32.246 --> 00:04:33.380
In fact, my perspective

00:04:33.381 --> 00:04:35.960
on what's happened in
the last say, 20, 30 years,

00:04:35.961 --> 00:04:38.090
it's a bit different from
the story you usually hear,

00:04:38.091 --> 00:04:40.330
that it's all about neural nets
to computer vision.

00:04:40.331 --> 00:04:42.115
So, I think first of all,

00:04:42.116 --> 00:04:44.210
20 years ago, it was
about the backend.

00:04:44.211 --> 00:04:45.650
The fraud detection was really

00:04:45.651 --> 00:04:47.270
really important in the
history of machine learning.

00:04:47.271 --> 00:04:48.740
It really worked and
it really changed

00:04:48.741 --> 00:04:49.980
the business model for Amazon

00:04:49.981 --> 00:04:51.395
and a lot of other in the banks.

00:04:51.396 --> 00:04:53.180
We wouldn't have
Amazon today if they

00:04:53.181 --> 00:04:55.060
hadn't been able to solve
it with Random Forest,

00:04:55.061 --> 00:04:57.750
actually, early
classification systems.

00:04:57.751 --> 00:04:59.205
Supply-chain management.

00:04:59.206 --> 00:05:00.520
How do you have an Amazon if you

00:05:00.521 --> 00:05:01.850
can't get all the
pieces together to

00:05:01.851 --> 00:05:03.380
build all the parts
for a billion products

00:05:03.381 --> 00:05:05.080
to serve hundreds of
millions of people?

00:05:05.081 --> 00:05:07.120
Well, you model all of that
with machine learning,

00:05:07.121 --> 00:05:08.775
that was done back then.

00:05:08.776 --> 00:05:10.980
So, after all those systems
had been built this,

00:05:10.981 --> 00:05:12.000
this was partly systems building.

00:05:12.001 --> 00:05:13.030
They had Random Forests,

00:05:13.031 --> 00:05:14.820
really built as
really good systems.

00:05:14.821 --> 00:05:16.200
People started to
think, what can we

00:05:16.201 --> 00:05:17.475
turn that onto the frontend.

00:05:17.476 --> 00:05:20.730
Can we have humans,
their data, in the loop?

00:05:20.731 --> 00:05:22.190
Can we build
recommendation systems,

00:05:22.191 --> 00:05:23.100
and so on, and so forth.

00:05:23.101 --> 00:05:25.125
So, I think, that's
what happened next.

00:05:25.126 --> 00:05:26.680
I think, what we're in right now,

00:05:26.681 --> 00:05:28.980
it's really what I
would call end-to-end.

00:05:28.981 --> 00:05:30.350
It's not that there's been

00:05:30.351 --> 00:05:31.790
a revolution in the technology,

00:05:31.791 --> 00:05:33.150
and yes, there's a lot
of data and all that.

00:05:33.151 --> 00:05:36.140
So, in some domains,
that's impressive,

00:05:36.141 --> 00:05:37.705
but it's not that different.

00:05:37.706 --> 00:05:38.690
What it really is that we have

00:05:38.691 --> 00:05:40.020
some end-to-end capabilities now.

00:05:40.021 --> 00:05:42.500
You can see you can build
almost as a commodity,

00:05:42.501 --> 00:05:44.080
a speech recognition system.

00:05:44.081 --> 00:05:46.400
It's an end-to-end system,
it takes in the waveform and

00:05:46.401 --> 00:05:49.330
outputs something like text.

00:05:49.331 --> 00:05:51.020
It doesn't solve the problem,

00:05:51.021 --> 00:05:52.250
but it's a useful commodity

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:53.200
for all kinds of other things.

00:05:53.201 --> 00:05:55.895
Similarly, with vision
problems and so on so forth.

00:05:55.896 --> 00:05:57.880
So, end-to-end, is
both good and bad.

00:05:57.881 --> 00:05:59.915
It allows you to build
a whole commodity,

00:05:59.916 --> 00:06:01.015
on the other hand,

00:06:01.016 --> 00:06:03.300
it couples everything together
in really complicated way,

00:06:03.301 --> 00:06:04.370
and it makes it hard
to actually build

00:06:04.371 --> 00:06:06.020
systems around those
kind of capabilities.

00:06:06.021 --> 00:06:08.355
So, I don't think it's
the model going forward.

00:06:08.356 --> 00:06:10.010
So, I think what's missing

00:06:10.011 --> 00:06:11.090
right now is actually
what we're seeing

00:06:11.091 --> 00:06:13.850
emerging, the
economic perspective.

00:06:13.851 --> 00:06:16.610
You make intelligence
systems by having lots of

00:06:16.611 --> 00:06:20.010
dumb local decisions in
a market with bidding,

00:06:20.011 --> 00:06:21.150
and pricing, and all of that,

00:06:21.151 --> 00:06:23.060
and out of that, starts
to emerge intelligence.

00:06:23.061 --> 00:06:25.110
So, first of all, I don't
like the word intelligence,

00:06:25.111 --> 00:06:27.080
I've said this in lots of talks
because we don't have it,

00:06:27.081 --> 00:06:28.950
we don't understand it,
and we're not really

00:06:28.951 --> 00:06:30.010
build it into our agents and

00:06:30.011 --> 00:06:31.350
all that, they are
not intelligent?

00:06:31.351 --> 00:06:32.960
They're taking in
data and mimicking

00:06:32.961 --> 00:06:34.610
what they see in the data, right?

00:06:34.611 --> 00:06:36.245
If we're ever going
to get intelligence,

00:06:36.246 --> 00:06:37.820
it's going to have to have
some economic principles,

00:06:37.821 --> 00:06:39.470
because a system that brings

00:06:39.471 --> 00:06:41.700
all the food into
San Francisco, every day,

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:43.400
to have every person
and every restaurant

00:06:43.401 --> 00:06:44.180
have all the food they need

00:06:44.181 --> 00:06:45.000
at the right place at right time,

00:06:45.001 --> 00:06:46.900
that's an amazingly
intelligent system.

00:06:46.901 --> 00:06:48.455
But as you know, there is

00:06:48.456 --> 00:06:50.300
no intelligence in
any point, in that system.

00:06:50.301 --> 00:06:51.380
Everybody's just
thinking about should

00:06:51.381 --> 00:06:52.520
I bring another head of lettuce

00:06:52.521 --> 00:06:55.595
into a particular street address.

00:06:55.596 --> 00:06:58.105
So, that's for me
what's happening.

00:06:58.106 --> 00:06:59.355
Now, I read an op-ed,

00:06:59.356 --> 00:07:00.650
recently, in this talk.

00:07:00.651 --> 00:07:02.330
It's inspired by that op-ed,

00:07:02.331 --> 00:07:03.470
if you haven't read that, I

00:07:03.471 --> 00:07:04.490
would love to have you read it.

00:07:04.491 --> 00:07:06.110
I do think that, it's important

00:07:06.111 --> 00:07:08.175
to start to use this word AI,

00:07:08.176 --> 00:07:09.615
in a more sensible way.

00:07:09.616 --> 00:07:12.885
It's being used as an intellectual
wild card in my view,

00:07:12.886 --> 00:07:14.480
when anybody doesn't understand

00:07:14.481 --> 00:07:16.645
what they're really talking
about, they say AI.

00:07:16.646 --> 00:07:18.480
I should say I think
Mark Zuckerberg

00:07:18.481 --> 00:07:19.660
has done that better
than anybody.

00:07:19.661 --> 00:07:21.110
Sitting in front of
Congress saying AI,

00:07:21.111 --> 00:07:23.350
whenever he didn't
know what else to say.

00:07:23.351 --> 00:07:26.015
I think we need to move beyond

00:07:26.016 --> 00:07:28.275
the usage of that phrase
in that way.

00:07:28.276 --> 00:07:30.620
All right. So, it was
originally McCarthy,

00:07:30.621 --> 00:07:31.510
and these others, who had

00:07:31.511 --> 00:07:32.820
this human imitative perspective.

00:07:32.821 --> 00:07:34.180
Would it be interesting, and fun,

00:07:34.181 --> 00:07:36.715
challenging, to make
a computer act like a human.

00:07:36.716 --> 00:07:38.945
So, let's try to
imitate the human.

00:07:38.946 --> 00:07:40.640
I don't think that's
what actually

00:07:40.641 --> 00:07:41.840
happened over the last 50 years,

00:07:41.841 --> 00:07:43.250
I don't think we've done
that, I don't think we can

00:07:43.251 --> 00:07:45.215
imitate humans at all.

00:07:45.216 --> 00:07:47.910
But what's really happened
is we've built IA systems.

00:07:47.911 --> 00:07:50.300
We built systems that
augment human intelligence,

00:07:50.301 --> 00:07:54.580
search engines, the translation,

00:07:54.581 --> 00:07:56.350
and so on, augment
our human intelligence.

00:07:56.351 --> 00:07:57.640
That will continue, and it

00:07:57.641 --> 00:07:59.290
uses data, and it uses actions,

00:07:59.291 --> 00:08:00.840
and it uses models,
and neural nets,

00:08:00.841 --> 00:08:03.300
or whatever you want to do that.

00:08:03.301 --> 00:08:05.330
But I think, the bigger picture
which we're seeing

00:08:05.331 --> 00:08:07.550
emerging is what I've
been calling II,

00:08:07.551 --> 00:08:09.240
again, none of this
is invented by me,

00:08:09.241 --> 00:08:10.980
I'm just trying to put
a highlight on it.

00:08:10.981 --> 00:08:12.420
It's Internet of Things,

00:08:12.421 --> 00:08:15.199
needs data analysis, and
statistics, and decision-making.

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:18.320
So, not just taking in data
and crunching it but making

00:08:18.321 --> 00:08:20.000
a large numbers of
federated decisions in

00:08:20.001 --> 00:08:22.435
a context of
large numbers of agents,

00:08:22.436 --> 00:08:24.410
hundreds of thousands of agents,

00:08:24.411 --> 00:08:25.980
simultaneously doing things.

00:08:25.981 --> 00:08:27.370
That's what we're
seeing emerging.

00:08:27.371 --> 00:08:29.445
That's not the classical
AI perspective,

00:08:29.446 --> 00:08:30.625
you have one agent making

00:08:30.626 --> 00:08:32.195
a decision with
all the data it has,

00:08:32.196 --> 00:08:33.990
and maybe making
a sequence at best.

00:08:33.991 --> 00:08:36.225
The real world is 100,000 agents

00:08:36.226 --> 00:08:38.240
making a bunch of
correlated decisions,

00:08:38.241 --> 00:08:40.400
which can be good or bad,
like the financial system,

00:08:40.401 --> 00:08:42.645
or the medical system, or
the transportation, or so,

00:08:42.646 --> 00:08:44.520
that's the world we
should be having

00:08:44.521 --> 00:08:45.680
more intelligence in and

00:08:45.681 --> 00:08:47.400
we're not really thinking
enough about that.

00:08:47.401 --> 00:08:48.990
Okay. So, let me just actually

00:08:48.991 --> 00:08:50.760
skip this slide, I'm
just saying about.

00:08:50.761 --> 00:08:52.395
I don't think with
human-imitative,

00:08:52.396 --> 00:08:54.965
were not as far along as
everyone seems to think.

00:08:54.966 --> 00:08:57.350
Even in vision, the systems

00:08:57.351 --> 00:08:58.990
work pretty well with
pictures on the Internet.

00:08:58.991 --> 00:09:01.185
They don't work well when
we got in the real world.

00:09:01.186 --> 00:09:02.720
You can train them up with

00:09:02.721 --> 00:09:04.630
huge amounts of labels,
and humans effort.

00:09:04.631 --> 00:09:08.280
So, Tesla's doing that to
build self-driving cars.

00:09:08.281 --> 00:09:10.010
That's fine, you can probably do

00:09:10.011 --> 00:09:11.570
something in
that particular niche.

00:09:11.571 --> 00:09:12.860
But we don't have systems that

00:09:12.861 --> 00:09:14.180
really understand
the visual world,

00:09:14.181 --> 00:09:15.435
the way we understand the verbs,

00:09:15.436 --> 00:09:17.330
and nouns in the world not

00:09:17.331 --> 00:09:19.970
just picking out
a few things and images.

00:09:19.971 --> 00:09:20.790
So, it's a great field,

00:09:20.791 --> 00:09:21.930
computer vision,
and it's not over.

00:09:21.931 --> 00:09:23.105
It's going to be decades before

00:09:23.106 --> 00:09:24.280
we start to get
somewhere with it.

00:09:24.281 --> 00:09:26.825
So, we're not there
yet, and that's great.

00:09:26.826 --> 00:09:28.355
But here's the main point,

00:09:28.356 --> 00:09:29.700
or it's one of my main points,

00:09:29.701 --> 00:09:30.860
is that I don't think

00:09:30.861 --> 00:09:33.010
human-imitative II
is the right goal.

00:09:33.011 --> 00:09:34.500
It should be to build

00:09:34.501 --> 00:09:36.060
really large-scale systems,
that take care in data,

00:09:36.061 --> 00:09:37.655
and make all kinds of decisions,

00:09:37.656 --> 00:09:39.490
and all kinds of scales,

00:09:39.491 --> 00:09:41.680
maybe for people, maybe for not.

