with computers.

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:20.020
Last little point on
my last two minutes,

00:22:20.021 --> 00:22:21.820
this is literally
advertisement is

00:22:21.821 --> 00:22:23.750
that when you start thinking
about multiple decisions,

00:22:23.751 --> 00:22:25.840
you do have to think about
sequences going forward,

00:22:25.841 --> 00:22:28.340
not just simultaneous
which I've emphasized.

00:22:28.341 --> 00:22:30.910
This is the area of
optimal control theory,

00:22:30.911 --> 00:22:32.330
it's now often
called reinforcement

00:22:32.331 --> 00:22:34.090
learning but it's just
optimal control theory.

00:22:34.091 --> 00:22:35.450
I really want to hope

00:22:35.451 --> 00:22:36.900
the system's committee
will start to

00:22:36.901 --> 00:22:38.330
resonate to the need to build

00:22:38.331 --> 00:22:40.525
systems that allow you
to do optimal control.

00:22:40.526 --> 00:22:43.620
So, now you have to face
the fact that lots of

00:22:43.621 --> 00:22:46.700
the tasks that you need
to run, I have very,

00:22:46.701 --> 00:22:48.400
very different lifetimes
that you're going to

00:22:48.401 --> 00:22:50.100
do a search process
through a sequence

00:22:50.101 --> 00:22:51.540
of control actions and

00:22:51.541 --> 00:22:53.970
sometimes you'll learn very
quickly that was a bad idea.

00:22:53.971 --> 00:22:56.010
Another particular path
through the tree of

00:22:56.011 --> 00:22:58.935
actions looks like a very
good idea for a long time.

00:22:58.936 --> 00:23:01.390
So, if you start
synchronous processes

00:23:01.391 --> 00:23:03.585
up and you have them all
wait for each other,

00:23:03.586 --> 00:23:05.270
you're going to be
waiting way too

00:23:05.271 --> 00:23:06.750
long in many situations.

00:23:06.751 --> 00:23:08.860
So, the mapreduce paradigm
is not appropriate

00:23:08.861 --> 00:23:11.675
for lots of these control tasks.

00:23:11.676 --> 00:23:13.490
So, let me just sort
of skip ahead here is

00:23:13.491 --> 00:23:15.355
just a little picture
to suggest this.

00:23:15.356 --> 00:23:18.225
If you're trying to make
a simulated robot perform,

00:23:18.226 --> 00:23:20.070
then what you're going to
do is try all the actions

00:23:20.071 --> 00:23:21.250
of all the joints and everything

00:23:21.251 --> 00:23:22.530
and try to build
a policy doing that

00:23:22.531 --> 00:23:24.220
you do lots of what
are called rollouts.

00:23:24.221 --> 00:23:26.620
AlphaGo did rollouts, most of

00:23:26.621 --> 00:23:28.860
the so-called success
reinforcement are all based on

00:23:28.861 --> 00:23:31.290
these rollouts in
a big, big tree.

00:23:31.291 --> 00:23:32.520
All right, so we
have been working

00:23:32.521 --> 00:23:33.730
on this system called Ray,

00:23:33.731 --> 00:23:35.190
there's several students and

00:23:35.191 --> 00:23:37.995
then John Stuart and myself
at Berkeley are leading this.

00:23:37.996 --> 00:23:40.660
It is all about trying to
do parallel version of

00:23:40.661 --> 00:23:44.525
Python where you can have
very heterogeneous tasks.

00:23:44.526 --> 00:23:45.960
Some of them are very
short, some are very

00:23:45.961 --> 00:23:47.515
long and there's
no synchrony anywhere.

00:23:47.516 --> 00:23:50.270
It's all a big data-flow graph
where the data,

00:23:50.271 --> 00:23:52.225
the graph itself is dynamic.

00:23:52.226 --> 00:23:56.940
All right, so, I like to
think about this as in

00:23:56.941 --> 00:23:59.080
Python you've got
functions and you've got

00:23:59.081 --> 00:24:01.655
objects and they're
both really useful.

00:24:01.656 --> 00:24:03.310
When people went
to MapReduce Jeff,

00:24:03.311 --> 00:24:04.630
Dean and others, they kept

00:24:04.631 --> 00:24:06.945
functions but they
threw out objects.

00:24:06.946 --> 00:24:09.440
It was all functional
programming.

00:24:09.441 --> 00:24:11.650
So, we want to restore
objects because we think

00:24:11.651 --> 00:24:12.670
they're really are
important so Ray

00:24:12.671 --> 00:24:13.915
is basically does that.

00:24:13.916 --> 00:24:15.625
Functions are now called tasks,

00:24:15.626 --> 00:24:17.990
they're distributed functions
and objects are now

00:24:17.991 --> 00:24:20.830
called actors in the
classical language of actors.

00:24:20.831 --> 00:24:22.310
So, they are stateful. So, we

00:24:22.311 --> 00:24:24.175
built a system that does this.

00:24:24.176 --> 00:24:26.720
Here's some performance,
I guess here

00:24:26.721 --> 00:24:27.910
60 nodes were able to get up to

00:24:27.911 --> 00:24:29.620
a million tasks per second.

00:24:29.621 --> 00:24:31.190
So, task of low light
weight and they

00:24:31.191 --> 00:24:32.885
can be used for these rollouts.

00:24:32.886 --> 00:24:34.555
It is a working system,

