00:28:00.751 --> 00:28:01.840
probably think about neural nets

00:28:01.841 --> 00:28:03.305
and you wouldn't be too wrong.

00:28:03.306 --> 00:28:04.860
One of the things
that kick this off is

00:28:04.861 --> 00:28:07.955
this Google system that

00:28:07.956 --> 00:28:10.890
shrink their language
translation system from 500,000

00:28:10.891 --> 00:28:14.080
lines of conventional C
plus plus code down to

00:28:14.081 --> 00:28:19.075
500 lines or so of
commodity machine learning code.

00:28:19.076 --> 00:28:22.500
And that seemed 500 lines of
code more or less actually,

00:28:22.501 --> 00:28:24.700
Facebook could replicate
it, Microsoft can replicate

00:28:24.701 --> 00:28:26.980
and a graduate student in
Germany could replicate.

00:28:26.981 --> 00:28:28.700
Something pretty exciting
at these as Mike

00:28:28.701 --> 00:28:30.240
was calling them end
to end applications

00:28:30.241 --> 00:28:32.300
are becoming
these insane commodity

00:28:32.301 --> 00:28:34.120
so quickly is really exciting.

00:28:34.121 --> 00:28:36.010
It changes where
you're going to put

00:28:36.011 --> 00:28:39.515
the high-value PhD people
actually doing any work.

00:28:39.516 --> 00:28:41.260
But what's been really
exciting as it's

00:28:41.261 --> 00:28:42.990
not just those end to

00:28:42.991 --> 00:28:47.470
end AI first,
Memel first applications.

00:28:47.471 --> 00:28:49.250
There's a bunch of stuff
that's going on that's really

00:28:49.251 --> 00:28:51.500
exciting that's taking
these principles

00:28:51.501 --> 00:28:52.965
and applying them
all over the place.

00:28:52.966 --> 00:28:54.665
I'll talk a little bit
about hollow clean that,

00:28:54.666 --> 00:28:56.265
you have Helios and
Thiel are doing.

00:28:56.266 --> 00:28:57.960
I'm taking these ideas
and applying

00:28:57.961 --> 00:28:59.550
them to problems
like data cleaning,

00:28:59.551 --> 00:29:00.890
and the extract transform

00:29:00.891 --> 00:29:02.680
load process, and
data integration.

00:29:02.681 --> 00:29:04.600
Andy Pablo is doing a bunch
of really cool work

00:29:04.601 --> 00:29:06.440
on tuning databases
and tuning systems

00:29:06.441 --> 00:29:08.000
using machine learning
and there's also

00:29:08.001 --> 00:29:10.220
cool work going on
in networks as well.

00:29:10.221 --> 00:29:12.565
So, this transition
where machine learning

00:29:12.566 --> 00:29:14.080
works well enough that it becomes

00:29:14.081 --> 00:29:15.775
a building block
for these systems,

00:29:15.776 --> 00:29:17.450
means we have to think through

00:29:17.451 --> 00:29:19.270
like how is software engineering
going to change?

00:29:19.271 --> 00:29:21.160
And that's why I think
this software 2.0 is

00:29:21.161 --> 00:29:23.270
an interesting paradigm.
So, what does it look like?

00:29:23.271 --> 00:29:25.350
Well, it's a vague buzz
word so let me try

00:29:25.351 --> 00:29:27.660
and put a little bit
of meat around it.

00:29:27.661 --> 00:29:29.830
So, conventionally software 1.0,

00:29:29.831 --> 00:29:31.250
you write code C plus plus

00:29:31.251 --> 00:29:34.575
Python code and you compile
down to an ISA, right?

00:29:34.576 --> 00:29:38.000
Machine-based instruction
maybe cuda compiling.

00:29:38.001 --> 00:29:40.775
What's going on in software
2.0 is two big changes.

00:29:40.776 --> 00:29:42.350
At the top and at the bottom.

00:29:42.351 --> 00:29:44.920
At the top what's changing
is the abstraction, right?

00:29:44.921 --> 00:29:46.730
How you actually build

00:29:46.731 --> 00:29:48.560
these applications and
what you do is you

00:29:48.561 --> 00:29:50.625
feed them training
data by enlarge.

00:29:50.626 --> 00:29:53.390
So, that's the input that
you're giving to these systems.

00:29:53.391 --> 00:29:55.180
Underneath what's really exciting

00:29:55.181 --> 00:29:56.890
to me the first time in
computing since I've

00:29:56.891 --> 00:29:58.350
been around in it is that

00:29:58.351 --> 00:30:00.195
instead of compiling
down to instructions,

00:30:00.196 --> 00:30:01.500
you're compiling down to like

00:30:01.501 --> 00:30:03.905
a blast like data flow like call.

00:30:03.906 --> 00:30:05.590
So, the hardware has a chance to

00:30:05.591 --> 00:30:07.810
reach up and actually
change as well.

00:30:07.811 --> 00:30:09.700
So, both the top and
the bottom of software

00:30:09.701 --> 00:30:12.025
changing that seems pretty
exciting and interesting.

00:30:12.026 --> 00:30:13.995
Okay, so, why is this happening?

00:30:13.996 --> 00:30:16.725
Now conventionally people
focus a lot on quality.

00:30:16.726 --> 00:30:18.260
They say, "Oh, I
built a system that

00:30:18.261 --> 00:30:20.960
has higher than human
accuracy at test x,

00:30:20.961 --> 00:30:25.245
y or z under some very,
very simple reasoning."

00:30:25.246 --> 00:30:27.820
But what's exciting is
that actually this is

00:30:27.821 --> 00:30:28.860
a way to build a lot of

00:30:28.861 --> 00:30:30.780
products with
high-quality faster.

00:30:30.781 --> 00:30:33.790
You have people who don't
have machine-learning PhDs,

00:30:33.791 --> 00:30:35.735
who maybe have basics of

00:30:35.736 --> 00:30:38.205
statistics being able
to build and deploy

00:30:38.206 --> 00:30:40.270
high-quality products
in a small amount of

00:30:40.271 --> 00:30:43.325
time and a speed to
develop them is amazing.

00:30:43.326 --> 00:30:45.780
Also the maintainability,
rather than having

00:30:45.781 --> 00:30:48.290
hand-tuned crazy
featurized systems,

00:30:48.291 --> 00:30:50.630
they're building these systems
that are basically getting

00:30:50.631 --> 00:30:52.230
all those low-level
details right for

00:30:52.231 --> 00:30:54.100
them so they can focus
on building products.

00:30:54.101 --> 00:30:55.600
So, that speed of execution

00:30:55.601 --> 00:30:57.415
is just dramatically different.

00:30:57.416 --> 00:30:59.550
What's also exciting is

00:30:59.551 --> 00:31:01.435
that the deployment
story has changed.

00:31:01.436 --> 00:31:03.445
So, neural nets have become

00:31:03.446 --> 00:31:05.380
weirdly enough like a new JVM.

00:31:05.381 --> 00:31:06.970
What I mean by that is that

00:31:06.971 --> 00:31:08.050
there the language that you

00:31:08.051 --> 00:31:10.165
compile down when you
want to ship code.

00:31:10.166 --> 00:31:12.270
So, the hyper scalars,
Microsoft very

00:31:12.271 --> 00:31:15.145
famously has a bunch of
great stuff like brainwave,

00:31:15.146 --> 00:31:17.580
you can compile down to
hardware for neural nets and

00:31:17.581 --> 00:31:18.610
once you do a bunch of

00:31:18.611 --> 00:31:20.360
problems are almost
solved for you.

00:31:20.361 --> 00:31:22.040
In particular, qualification

00:31:22.041 --> 00:31:23.190
this process you go through at

00:31:23.191 --> 00:31:24.530
any other hyper scalars
where you have

00:31:24.531 --> 00:31:26.670
code you want to ship
it to the data center,

00:31:26.671 --> 00:31:28.240
it gets much easier if

00:31:28.241 --> 00:31:30.095
you've compiled down
to our neural net.

00:31:30.096 --> 00:31:32.185
There's no allocation
on the critical path,

00:31:32.186 --> 00:31:33.630
it's using a set of operators

00:31:33.631 --> 00:31:35.180
that people have pre-qualified,

00:31:35.181 --> 00:31:38.310
it's changing in that incentive
changes what engineers

00:31:38.311 --> 00:31:39.800
want to build because they
can get their software

00:31:39.801 --> 00:31:41.500
out there so much faster.

00:31:41.501 --> 00:31:42.830
Now in a parallel life with

00:31:42.831 --> 00:31:45.080
Kunle the father
multi-core and a guy

00:31:45.081 --> 00:31:46.780
named Rodrigo Liang who led

00:31:46.781 --> 00:31:49.365
1,000 person chip
group at Oracle.

00:31:49.366 --> 00:31:51.380
We are actually some of
our research has being

00:31:51.381 --> 00:31:52.540
commercialized to actually build

00:31:52.541 --> 00:31:54.460
hardware for ML, which
is really exciting.

00:31:54.461 --> 00:31:56.880
They're all underneath changes

00:31:56.881 --> 00:31:59.245
about how today's
neural nets are trained

00:31:59.246 --> 00:32:01.230
that are allowing hardware to

00:32:01.231 --> 00:32:03.080
really radically change and

00:32:03.081 --> 00:32:04.300
come up and meet
this abstraction.

00:32:04.301 --> 00:32:05.845
But that's not what I'm
going to talk about,

00:32:05.846 --> 00:32:08.185
I'm going to focus more on
that level of abstraction,

00:32:08.186 --> 00:32:09.630
about how programming
and building

00:32:09.631 --> 00:32:11.755
these things is really exciting.

00:32:11.756 --> 00:32:15.715
So, the way we got into this
was about two years ago.

00:32:15.716 --> 00:32:17.455
We looked at this landscape of

00:32:17.456 --> 00:32:20.060
every week if you
remember in sort of 2016,

00:32:20.061 --> 00:32:22.030
you would wake up
and look at archive,

00:32:22.031 --> 00:32:23.290
and something else
would be posted:

00:32:23.291 --> 00:32:25.180
this neural net does that,
this neural net does that.

00:32:25.181 --> 00:32:27.630
It was a mad dash for innovation.

00:32:27.631 --> 00:32:29.150
We looked at that and we said,

00:32:29.151 --> 00:32:30.750
"Well, a lot of people are
working on that problem.

00:32:30.751 --> 00:32:32.530
We want to work on
something else."

00:32:32.531 --> 00:32:35.555
We bet that that was going
to become a commodity.

00:32:35.556 --> 00:32:37.140
So, what we thought
was going to happen,

00:32:37.141 --> 00:32:38.910
we didn't know
the term Software 2.0,

00:32:38.911 --> 00:32:40.320
but we thought that deep learning

00:32:40.321 --> 00:32:41.660
would basically plateau and hit

00:32:41.661 --> 00:32:43.610
a commodity point
where people could

00:32:43.611 --> 00:32:46.645
download and build software
with it pretty reliably.

00:32:46.646 --> 00:32:48.290
So, we published this paper in

00:32:48.291 --> 00:32:50.570
mid 2016 that we called
Data Programming.

00:32:50.571 --> 00:32:51.880
They started to think about what

00:32:51.881 --> 00:32:53.210
was happening up the stack,

00:32:53.211 --> 00:32:54.640
and I'll tell you about the
software that we've been

00:32:54.641 --> 00:32:56.210
building to implement this idea

00:32:56.211 --> 00:32:57.740
over the last couple of years.

00:32:57.741 --> 00:33:00.140
Now, the key observation was that

00:33:00.141 --> 00:33:02.210
if you looked where engineers
were spending their time,

00:33:02.211 --> 00:33:04.645
they weren't spending
their time building new models.

00:33:04.646 --> 00:33:06.370
They weren't spending
their time actually

00:33:06.371 --> 00:33:08.285
tweaking the underlying systems.

00:33:08.286 --> 00:33:09.160
They actually weren't even

00:33:09.161 --> 00:33:10.400
worried that much about run-time.

00:33:10.401 --> 00:33:11.974
They just put it on some GPUs,

00:33:11.975 --> 00:33:13.310
maybe, they go get a coffee.

00:33:13.311 --> 00:33:15.535
Where they were spending
all their time was basically

00:33:15.536 --> 00:33:18.665
creating and feeding
training data to these systems.

00:33:18.666 --> 00:33:21.225
We thought, because we have
sort of a theoretical bent,

00:33:21.226 --> 00:33:22.960
why isn't there
any mathematical structure

00:33:22.961 --> 00:33:24.620
around sort of creating
training data?

00:33:24.621 --> 00:33:26.000
Why does sort of Machine Learning

00:33:26.001 --> 00:33:27.255
conventionally start with

00:33:27.256 --> 00:33:30.400
data coming from god herself
and showing up in XY pairs?

00:33:30.401 --> 00:33:32.760
Even though all those XY pairs
were generated by

00:33:32.761 --> 00:33:35.535
a huge system that's consuming
all of people's time.

00:33:35.536 --> 00:33:37.620
So, what we decided to
do is try and build

00:33:37.621 --> 00:33:40.250
both mathematical and
systems models of what was

00:33:40.251 --> 00:33:41.700
going on there with the hope

00:33:41.701 --> 00:33:43.190
of both better understanding it,

00:33:43.191 --> 00:33:45.410
and also hopefully
accelerating that process.

00:33:45.411 --> 00:33:48.460
That's where this thing
Snorkel was born.

00:33:48.461 --> 00:33:50.400
Now, very quickly, we stumbled on

00:33:50.401 --> 00:33:51.880
one little technical nugget,

00:33:51.881 --> 00:33:53.360
but there are many things
in this space

00:33:53.361 --> 00:33:54.430
and I'll try and highlight them.

00:33:54.431 --> 00:33:56.930
It was this key idea
that if you're going to

00:33:56.931 --> 00:34:01.245
program Software 2.0 by
getting training data,

00:34:01.246 --> 00:34:04.265
then maybe what was
holding you back in

00:34:04.266 --> 00:34:06.045
some situations was leveraging

00:34:06.046 --> 00:34:08.020
all the sources that
were available to you,

00:34:08.021 --> 00:34:09.680
of various different quality.

00:34:09.681 --> 00:34:11.100
So, what we started to do

00:34:11.101 --> 00:34:12.450
or develop program abstractions,

00:34:12.451 --> 00:34:14.070
really simple
program abstractions,

00:34:14.071 --> 00:34:15.450
that could just talk about

00:34:15.451 --> 00:34:18.485
different sources that were
informing your training data.

00:34:18.486 --> 00:34:20.730
The mathematical
questions were to be able

00:34:20.731 --> 00:34:22.580
to learn the accuracy
of those sources

00:34:22.581 --> 00:34:24.435
and the correlations
among those sources

00:34:24.436 --> 00:34:27.205
but without any
conventional training data.

00:34:27.206 --> 00:34:29.820
Now, when we started this,

00:34:29.821 --> 00:34:31.365
people were skeptical
saying like well,

00:34:31.366 --> 00:34:34.040
we'll build bigger neural nets
and that will be the way

00:34:34.041 --> 00:34:36.125
to higher-quality on
state-of-the-art tasks,

00:34:36.126 --> 00:34:36.805
and we didn't care.

00:34:36.806 --> 00:34:38.890
We said, "Well we're
going to go after people

00:34:38.891 --> 00:34:42.290
who were just deploying
these things inside companies,

00:34:42.291 --> 00:34:44.505
who were medical people
and all the rest,

00:34:44.506 --> 00:34:46.285
who just needed to
build these products.

00:34:46.286 --> 00:34:48.110
But what's been interesting
is over the last year,

00:34:48.111 --> 00:34:50.105
the state-of-the-art for
things like ImageNet,

00:34:50.106 --> 00:34:52.945
even a straight head benchmark,

00:34:52.946 --> 00:34:55.680
have both come out of
tools that basically

00:34:55.681 --> 00:34:58.430
allowed you to do more
weak supervision on top.

00:34:58.431 --> 00:34:59.945
Facebook published
this thing about using

00:34:59.946 --> 00:35:01.780
hashtags in various
different ways,

00:35:01.781 --> 00:35:03.700
the AutoAugment people
are doing something

00:35:03.701 --> 00:35:04.700
really cool that followed up on

00:35:04.701 --> 00:35:05.830
some stuff that we were up to,

00:35:05.831 --> 00:35:07.370
about learning data augmentations

00:35:07.371 --> 00:35:08.710
and learning composition.

00:35:08.711 --> 00:35:11.420
The point is that
these other signals to build

00:35:11.421 --> 00:35:13.120
real products always exist

00:35:13.121 --> 00:35:14.685
when you go to build
a real product,

00:35:14.686 --> 00:35:16.670
but the benchmarks
don't capture them.

00:35:16.671 --> 00:35:19.140
So, this engineering
challenge of bringing in

00:35:19.141 --> 00:35:21.270
more and more signal
seems to actually

00:35:21.271 --> 00:35:23.920
be catching on even
for the benchmarks.

00:35:23.921 --> 00:35:25.740
Another thing that
I'll highlight here,

00:35:25.741 --> 00:35:26.620
Ihab is in the audience,

00:35:26.621 --> 00:35:27.530
you should definitely
talk to him.

00:35:27.531 --> 00:35:28.950
He's the real expert here.

00:35:28.951 --> 00:35:30.350
But on even on things like

00:35:30.351 --> 00:35:32.185
conventional things
like data cleaning,

00:35:32.186 --> 00:35:33.865
being able to apply
these ideas of

00:35:33.866 --> 00:35:35.270
weak supervision and automatic

00:35:35.271 --> 00:35:36.630
feature generation gives you

00:35:36.631 --> 00:35:38.750
tremendous benefit in terms of

00:35:38.751 --> 00:35:41.115
precision and recall compared
to the state of the art,

00:35:41.116 --> 00:35:42.350
and Ihab is definitely expert.

00:35:42.351 --> 00:35:43.520
He's back over there.
You should talk

00:35:43.521 --> 00:35:44.670
to him about HoloClean.

00:35:44.671 --> 00:35:46.160
They have a big website and

00:35:46.161 --> 00:35:47.670
Theo is working on this as well.

00:35:47.671 --> 00:35:50.480
So the point is, like this
stuff is coming but we don't

00:35:50.481 --> 00:35:52.015
really have any
engineering abstractions

00:35:52.016 --> 00:35:53.780
for it, and that's what
I'd like to build.

00:35:53.781 --> 00:35:56.970
So, we started building Snorkel
led by two great folks,

00:35:56.971 --> 00:35:57.730
one is Stephen Bach and

00:35:57.731 --> 00:35:59.165
another great student,
Alex Ratner.

00:35:59.166 --> 00:36:00.700
He's on the market this year,

00:36:00.701 --> 00:36:02.220
you'll see his picture
many times.

00:36:02.221 --> 00:36:03.885
There is his picture.

00:36:03.886 --> 00:36:05.060
So, Stephen Bach is now doing

00:36:05.061 --> 00:36:06.530
some stuff at Google
related to this,

00:36:06.531 --> 00:36:08.800
and Alex Ratner really
led this project as well.

00:36:08.801 --> 00:36:10.955
He's on the market,
please hire him.

00:36:10.956 --> 00:36:13.550
Now, what I want you to show you.

00:36:13.551 --> 00:36:15.780
First thing is
programming is changing.

00:36:15.781 --> 00:36:17.890
So, not only can we
deskill many tasks,

00:36:17.891 --> 00:36:19.095
that was our original goal.

00:36:19.096 --> 00:36:21.675
Make it easier for more people
to use machine learning.

00:36:21.676 --> 00:36:24.240
We also realized very quickly
in our conversations with

00:36:24.241 --> 00:36:26.390
folks in the industry
that even the experts,

00:36:26.391 --> 00:36:29.965
the seven-figure-salary folks
coming out of grad school,

00:36:29.966 --> 00:36:32.195
they were having
similar kinds of problems,

00:36:32.196 --> 00:36:33.650
and that even experts were

00:36:33.651 --> 00:36:35.270
building and stumbling
on these problems of

00:36:35.271 --> 00:36:36.715
training data generation and data

00:36:36.716 --> 00:36:38.960
augmentation so we
wanted to study those.

00:36:38.961 --> 00:36:41.270
The second thing
is, if you look at

00:36:41.271 --> 00:36:43.670
classical questions around
supervised machine learning,

00:36:43.671 --> 00:36:44.930
once you start to realize

00:36:44.931 --> 00:36:48.465
that you don't have labeled data
that you can trust,

00:36:48.466 --> 00:36:50.090
many different things actually

00:36:50.091 --> 00:36:51.260
change underneath the covers,

00:36:51.261 --> 00:36:53.030
about how you estimate
source accuracy

00:36:53.031 --> 00:36:54.405
and correlations
and all the rest.

00:36:54.406 --> 00:36:56.940
There's a bunch of really
classical theoretical work that

00:36:56.941 --> 00:36:58.240
goes on underneath the covers

00:36:58.241 --> 00:36:59.770
that we're really excited about.

00:36:59.771 --> 00:37:02.535
The point is also there's
a lot more to do,

00:37:02.536 --> 00:37:05.225
all kinds of things in
a pipeline: data augmentation,

00:37:05.226 --> 00:37:08.000
ETL can be modeled
and potentially,

00:37:08.001 --> 00:37:09.795
make it easier to
build applications.

00:37:09.796 --> 00:37:12.030
I don't claim that we have
solutions that are even

00:37:12.031 --> 00:37:14.480
very good but there are
first cut on these solutions,

00:37:14.481 --> 00:37:16.540
and there's a lot more to do
to be able to build these

00:37:16.541 --> 00:37:19.360
end-to-end machine learning
kinds of products.

00:37:19.361 --> 00:37:22.300
So as I mentioned, this is
mainly a trolling slide,

00:37:22.301 --> 00:37:23.585
data was the New Oil,

00:37:23.586 --> 00:37:25.165
so training data is
the New New Oil.

00:37:25.166 --> 00:37:26.875
I have no real idea why,

00:37:26.876 --> 00:37:28.760
but it is really
a fundamental problem in

00:37:28.761 --> 00:37:29.845
machine learning that you

00:37:29.846 --> 00:37:31.255
need high-quality training data.

00:37:31.256 --> 00:37:32.740
As Mike talked about, most of

00:37:32.741 --> 00:37:34.375
the applications of
deep learning or almost

00:37:34.376 --> 00:37:38.280
all of them really come through
a lot of supervised data.

00:37:38.281 --> 00:37:41.310
As I mentioned,
traditional view is

00:37:41.311 --> 00:37:42.770
that this data just falls

00:37:42.771 --> 00:37:44.235
out of the sky and
you're given it.

00:37:44.236 --> 00:37:45.910
But every application that

00:37:45.911 --> 00:37:47.915
really generates dollars
that I'm aware of,

00:37:47.916 --> 00:37:50.955
that training data was
created by a process: people,

00:37:50.956 --> 00:37:53.805
software, all kinds of
things go into that process.

00:37:53.806 --> 00:37:55.680
The key idea that I want
to show you is that

00:37:55.681 --> 00:37:57.785
by modeling that process,
that provenance,

00:37:57.786 --> 00:38:00.130
Mike used the same term as well,

00:38:00.131 --> 00:38:02.165
modeling the provenance
of that process

00:38:02.166 --> 00:38:03.835
actually can improve the quality.

00:38:03.836 --> 00:38:05.480
That's the art project of it.

00:38:05.481 --> 00:38:07.030
Is it possible that going and

00:38:07.031 --> 00:38:08.230
understanding that pipeline

00:38:08.231 --> 00:38:09.655
and mathematically modeling it,

00:38:09.656 --> 00:38:12.000
does anything for
the downstream task?

00:38:12.001 --> 00:38:13.375
Actually the answer is yes,

00:38:13.376 --> 00:38:14.620
and that's what we've
been working on for

00:38:14.621 --> 00:38:16.485
the last couple of
years in this project.

00:38:16.486 --> 00:38:18.490
So, that's what Snorkel
is, it's all online.

00:38:18.491 --> 00:38:19.780
We've been running
workshops about it.

00:38:19.781 --> 00:38:21.160
As you'll see, there
are a bunch of users

00:38:21.161 --> 00:38:22.880
for it which is exciting,

00:38:22.881 --> 00:38:24.155
and we're modeling
these trainings,

00:38:24.156 --> 00:38:26.720
that creation process, and
data augmentation process.

00:38:26.721 --> 00:38:28.650
There's a blog and
all the rest of that crap.

00:38:28.651 --> 00:38:30.845
All right, so how does it work?

00:38:30.846 --> 00:38:32.265
I won't go into
too much technical detail

00:38:32.266 --> 00:38:33.545
because we have
really short talk.

00:38:33.546 --> 00:38:34.980
But please ask me,
please check out

00:38:34.981 --> 00:38:36.970
the blog, and send feedback.

00:38:36.971 --> 00:38:39.120
The first and simplest
abstraction that we

00:38:39.121 --> 00:38:41.690
could use was this idea
of just functions.

00:38:41.691 --> 00:38:42.925
So the idea was that,

00:38:42.926 --> 00:38:45.360
what you're going to have is
a bunch of maps that took

00:38:45.361 --> 00:38:48.755
your training set in and
then mapped to every point.

00:38:48.756 --> 00:38:50.875
You can imagine it's binary
classification just to make

00:38:50.876 --> 00:38:53.635
the discussion simpler:
yes, no, or I don't know.

00:38:53.636 --> 00:38:55.190
I don't know it's critically
important by the way.

00:38:55.191 --> 00:38:55.830
That's where a lot of

00:38:55.831 --> 00:38:57.685
the interesting
intellectual work happens.

00:38:57.686 --> 00:38:59.740
You would provide these functions

00:38:59.741 --> 00:39:01.945
and they would
generate noisy labels.

00:39:01.946 --> 00:39:03.605
You don't know their accuracy,

00:39:03.606 --> 00:39:05.925
you don't know the
correlations of the labels,

00:39:05.926 --> 00:39:07.570
but you know that
they're not probably

00:39:07.571 --> 00:39:09.385
not of perfect quality.

00:39:09.386 --> 00:39:11.820
Then, the second piece
is that you would

00:39:11.821 --> 00:39:13.750
model those labeling
functions outputs,

00:39:13.751 --> 00:39:14.890
and we chose to model them as

00:39:14.891 --> 00:39:17.085
graphical models basically
for historical reasons and,

00:39:17.086 --> 00:39:18.965
because we could prove
theorems about them.

00:39:18.966 --> 00:39:20.750
You would try and figure out in

00:39:20.751 --> 00:39:22.530
those graphical models what

00:39:22.531 --> 00:39:23.875
are the parameters
of those models?

00:39:23.876 --> 00:39:25.255
How accurate is every source?

00:39:25.256 --> 00:39:27.515
How correlated is it with
those different pieces?

00:39:27.516 --> 00:39:28.960
But the twist is, instead of

00:39:28.961 --> 00:39:30.720
seeing a bunch of observations,

00:39:30.721 --> 00:39:33.185
we would only see when
they agreed and disagreed,

00:39:33.186 --> 00:39:34.430
and we would try to come up with

00:39:34.431 --> 00:39:36.105
basically statistical models that

00:39:36.106 --> 00:39:37.345
would allow us to conclude.

00:39:37.346 --> 00:39:39.645
We could learn
those parameters provably,

00:39:39.646 --> 00:39:40.875
and that's what basically the

00:39:40.876 --> 00:39:41.980
program that we've been on for

00:39:41.981 --> 00:39:43.815
the last couple of
years where you don't

00:39:43.816 --> 00:39:46.765
actually get to see the value y.

00:39:46.766 --> 00:39:48.845
Then finally, to take advantage

00:39:48.846 --> 00:39:50.430
of all this nice deep
learning stuff,

00:39:50.431 --> 00:39:51.630
we would then label

00:39:51.631 --> 00:39:53.540
the entire training
set with accuracy.

00:39:53.541 --> 00:39:54.635
So instead of getting a zero,

00:39:54.636 --> 00:39:57.060
one or minus one, you get
a probability distribution,

00:39:57.061 --> 00:39:58.875
and we would pass
that to a deep model,

00:39:58.876 --> 00:40:01.120
and we would hope that that
uncertainty quantification

00:40:01.121 --> 00:40:02.650
would allow it to
do a better job.

00:40:02.651 --> 00:40:03.910
It would know to
trust some points

00:40:03.911 --> 00:40:05.795
more than other points.

00:40:05.796 --> 00:40:08.530
Now, I don't have slides here,

00:40:08.531 --> 00:40:10.630
but you can definitely look
on the website about this.

00:40:10.631 --> 00:40:12.410
This actually surprisingly has

00:40:12.411 --> 00:40:13.590
big impacts on things like

00:40:13.591 --> 00:40:15.375
natural language
processing applications,

00:40:15.376 --> 00:40:17.530
cross-model applications,
all kinds of

00:40:17.531 --> 00:40:19.960
different places where we've
demonstrated this on NLP,

00:40:19.961 --> 00:40:21.725
in vision, and images.

00:40:21.726 --> 00:40:23.465
This quantification
of uncertainty

00:40:23.466 --> 00:40:25.365
actually seems to
help in some cases.

00:40:25.366 --> 00:40:26.750
That it helps average great,

00:40:26.751 --> 00:40:28.155
we're really excited about that.

00:40:28.156 --> 00:40:29.495
It never seems to hurt,

00:40:29.496 --> 00:40:30.675
and that's really the theory

00:40:30.676 --> 00:40:32.065
that we've been
trying to work out.

00:40:32.066 --> 00:40:34.660
More importantly, it
changes how people program.

00:40:34.661 --> 00:40:36.150
So we've been basically giving

00:40:36.151 --> 00:40:37.660
demos to people and allowing

00:40:37.661 --> 00:40:38.845
them to write code on it

00:40:38.846 --> 00:40:40.505
and a bunch of folks at industry,

00:40:40.506 --> 00:40:42.675
in companies, I'll put
some logos up later,

00:40:42.676 --> 00:40:44.825
we're actually using this
to build applications.

00:40:44.826 --> 00:40:46.400
So, what we're after
here is trying to

00:40:46.401 --> 00:40:48.600
understand how this changes
the programming model.

00:40:48.601 --> 00:40:50.215
Again, we're at
the early days here.

00:40:50.216 --> 00:40:52.720
We don't exactly claim that
we have the right thing,

00:40:52.721 --> 00:40:54.020
but there's a lot that can

00:40:54.021 --> 00:40:55.860
change when you start
to view sort of

00:40:55.861 --> 00:40:58.810
your way of programming and
as generating training data.

00:40:58.811 --> 00:41:00.355
So, let me give you

00:41:00.356 --> 00:41:02.915
one example of something
that changes concretely,

00:41:02.916 --> 00:41:05.390
it's actually a real person
who was building one of

00:41:05.391 --> 00:41:06.810
the early versions
of the system using

00:41:06.811 --> 00:41:08.365
a technique called
Distant Supervision.

00:41:08.366 --> 00:41:10.525
So what they were doing is they

00:41:10.526 --> 00:41:11.520
first wrote a bunch of these in

00:41:11.521 --> 00:41:13.165
our terminology
labeling functions,

00:41:13.166 --> 00:41:15.145
and they were very highly
accurate, very precise.

00:41:15.146 --> 00:41:18.730
They labeled to say 10,000
points at 90 percent accuracy.

00:41:18.731 --> 00:41:21.445
Then, they came along and said,

00:41:21.446 --> 00:41:22.995
"I don't have enough
coverage in my model."

00:41:22.996 --> 00:41:25.435
What I need to do is label
a bunch more points,

00:41:25.436 --> 00:41:28.615
and they wrote a lower-quality
labeling function.

00:41:28.616 --> 00:41:29.925
They label it and
say a million points

00:41:29.926 --> 00:41:31.575
at 60 percent accuracy.

00:41:31.576 --> 00:41:33.945
Now conventionally, what
you would do is basically

00:41:33.946 --> 00:41:37.260
assume that all those labels
came from a uniform source.

00:41:37.261 --> 00:41:38.950
As a result, what you
would have without

00:41:38.951 --> 00:41:40.920
modeling that lineage
or provenance was

00:41:40.921 --> 00:41:42.740
a set of data that
looked like it was

00:41:42.741 --> 00:41:44.695
about 60.3 percent accurate.

00:41:44.696 --> 00:41:46.585
You wouldn't remember if you like

00:41:46.586 --> 00:41:48.860
that you have
that high accuracy pool.

00:41:48.861 --> 00:41:50.420
Unfortunately, that will make

00:41:50.421 --> 00:41:52.640
your overall model
quality go down,

00:41:52.641 --> 00:41:54.975
which is extremely
bad and frustrating

00:41:54.976 --> 00:41:57.170
as a user of this programming
model because you gave

00:41:57.171 --> 00:41:59.640
the system more information
and then your quality to

00:41:59.641 --> 00:42:02.800
the eye looks much much
worse, very very frustrating.

00:42:02.801 --> 00:42:04.325
But, of course, as I've told you,

00:42:04.326 --> 00:42:05.700
the fix is obvious.

00:42:05.701 --> 00:42:07.960
If you remember where
these came from,

00:42:07.961 --> 00:42:09.765
even if I didn't tell
you the accuracies,

00:42:09.766 --> 00:42:11.580
you'd have a better job

00:42:11.581 --> 00:42:13.835
of estimating which training
points you should trust,

00:42:13.836 --> 00:42:15.780
which were high-precision
which were low-precision.

00:42:15.781 --> 00:42:17.380
The art is to figure out

00:42:17.381 --> 00:42:19.530
automatically what
those accuracies

00:42:19.531 --> 00:42:21.875
are without seeing training data.

00:42:21.876 --> 00:42:24.030
So, it's a very
frustrating experience

00:42:24.031 --> 00:42:25.200
for users but it's the kinds of

00:42:25.201 --> 00:42:28.050
things that we've been basically
publishing papers about.

00:42:28.051 --> 00:42:30.680
So without going into
too much technical detail

00:42:30.681 --> 00:42:33.070
here, basically, we can
provably learn the accuracy

00:42:33.071 --> 00:42:34.330
of these labeling functions,

00:42:34.331 --> 00:42:36.165
even among correlations
among them,

00:42:36.166 --> 00:42:37.580
without hand-labeled data, and

00:42:37.581 --> 00:42:39.060
even features under
which they work.

00:42:39.061 --> 00:42:41.260
We can actually surface
features of them.

00:42:41.261 --> 00:42:43.660
This is a sort of long, we're
not at the end of this,

00:42:43.661 --> 00:42:45.180
we continually keep publishing

00:42:45.181 --> 00:42:47.680
essentially improvements
to when we can learn,

00:42:47.681 --> 00:42:50.275
and can we match
supervise scaling rates.

00:42:50.276 --> 00:42:52.650
In many cases, we can match
supervise scaling rates.

00:42:52.651 --> 00:42:55.100
In some places, we can
estimate correlations.

00:42:55.101 --> 00:42:56.590
We may actually improve some old

00:42:56.591 --> 00:42:57.860
supervised bounds of learning

00:42:57.861 --> 00:42:59.010
graphical models structure with

00:42:59.011 --> 00:43:01.460
this over the summer,
which is really exciting.

00:43:01.461 --> 00:43:03.965
So this is the goal: change
the programming model,

00:43:03.966 --> 00:43:06.495
and figure out what we can
offload and make automatic,

00:43:06.496 --> 00:43:07.970
and understand
the minimal statistical

00:43:07.971 --> 00:43:09.390
assumptions that allow us

00:43:09.391 --> 00:43:10.875
to do basically carry out

00:43:10.876 --> 00:43:12.855
this program, and that's
what we've been after.

00:43:12.856 --> 00:43:14.545
One thing that was just posted,

00:43:14.546 --> 00:43:15.870
it's been in use with a bunch of

00:43:15.871 --> 00:43:17.290
our collaborators is doing

00:43:17.291 --> 00:43:20.910
Multi-task Supervision stuff
and Multi-Task Learning.

00:43:20.911 --> 00:43:22.910
It turns out real
applications don't have

00:43:22.911 --> 00:43:25.450
just one classified output

00:43:25.451 --> 00:43:27.320
by learning across
multiple tasks.

00:43:27.321 --> 00:43:28.680
You can do a much better and more

00:43:28.681 --> 00:43:30.355
robust job of dealing with air,

00:43:30.356 --> 00:43:32.540
this is something that
we've put out and actually,

00:43:32.541 --> 00:43:34.790
Mike mentioned Alibaba,
these guys are amazing.

00:43:34.791 --> 00:43:36.555
If you're not paying
attention to Alibaba

00:43:36.556 --> 00:43:39.275
compared to a US Company,
they're amazingly open.

00:43:39.276 --> 00:43:41.000
They took our software when it

00:43:41.001 --> 00:43:42.885
was like as alpha as possible,

00:43:42.886 --> 00:43:45.500
used it, showed us where it
was used in their pipeline,

00:43:45.501 --> 00:43:46.610
gave us a bunch of feedback,

00:43:46.611 --> 00:43:47.960
and actually, in part,

00:43:47.961 --> 00:43:49.730
some of the reason
we prioritize doing

00:43:49.731 --> 00:43:51.300
the MTL stuff was because of

00:43:51.301 --> 00:43:53.715
that amazing technical
feedback they gave us.

00:43:53.716 --> 00:43:55.250
So, this is something
called metal.

00:43:55.251 --> 00:43:56.410
It was just released and

00:43:56.411 --> 00:43:58.455
the theory has gotten
dramatically cleaned up.

00:43:58.456 --> 00:44:00.815
It's a little bit more
fancy than it was before.

00:44:00.816 --> 00:44:02.515
But mathematically,
it's much nicer,

00:44:02.516 --> 00:44:05.090
and it's practically quite
useful because you can build

00:44:05.091 --> 00:44:07.610
these tasks at multiple
different levels of abstraction.

00:44:07.611 --> 00:44:09.120
You can do parsing,

00:44:09.121 --> 00:44:11.960
natural tagging for types,

00:44:11.961 --> 00:44:14.230
you can do question
understanding all

00:44:14.231 --> 00:44:16.580
in one model and
where one model is

00:44:16.581 --> 00:44:18.770
weak another one could be
strong and you can actually

00:44:18.771 --> 00:44:21.465
characterize this and why
it improves generalization.

00:44:21.466 --> 00:44:22.650
A lot of that was done

00:44:22.651 --> 00:44:24.590
10,15 years ago when
people were looking

00:44:24.591 --> 00:44:28.130
at how multitask learning
was actually beneficial.

00:44:28.131 --> 00:44:29.370
But in this new regime where you

00:44:29.371 --> 00:44:30.690
don't trust your training data,

00:44:30.691 --> 00:44:32.790
there are a couple of
twists and turns there but

00:44:32.791 --> 00:44:35.295
it's a great software
abstraction, okay?

00:44:35.296 --> 00:44:36.600
As I mentioned, the software is

00:44:36.601 --> 00:44:38.740
only about two years
old but here are some

00:44:38.741 --> 00:44:40.380
of the users and
sponsors and there are

00:44:40.381 --> 00:44:42.585
actually many users who
are not on the slides.

00:44:42.586 --> 00:44:44.220
We run these workshops and

00:44:44.221 --> 00:44:46.330
people are able to build
something with it.

00:44:46.331 --> 00:44:48.130
Again, this is not
great software,

00:44:48.131 --> 00:44:49.810
this is graduate
students stuff but

00:44:49.811 --> 00:44:51.670
people are able to pick
it up and actually use

00:44:51.671 --> 00:44:53.160
it because it fulfills

00:44:53.161 --> 00:44:55.455
this niche that there's
no programming abstraction.

00:44:55.456 --> 00:44:56.935
So, our bar is pretty low

00:44:56.936 --> 00:44:59.115
because there's nothing
else that's allowing you to

00:44:59.116 --> 00:45:00.360
think about that training set

00:45:00.361 --> 00:45:02.330
generation process
and I hope some folks

00:45:02.331 --> 00:45:03.820
get excited in the audience
and build something

00:45:03.821 --> 00:45:06.460
much better than what we have.

00:45:06.660 --> 00:45:09.070
I was asked to give a hot take in

00:45:09.071 --> 00:45:11.835
my last two minutes
and I would so I will.

00:45:11.836 --> 00:45:13.840
So, one of the things
Mike was talking

00:45:13.841 --> 00:45:16.080
about the difference between
AI and machine learning.

00:45:16.081 --> 00:45:17.360
I think sometimes even learning

00:45:17.361 --> 00:45:19.590
itself is not what
people actually want in

00:45:19.591 --> 00:45:22.600
industry or it's overused
in a way that maybe leads

00:45:22.601 --> 00:45:23.930
them astray and there's nothing

00:45:23.931 --> 00:45:26.620
wrong with learning,
it's fantastic.

00:45:26.621 --> 00:45:29.750
The generalization results
and all the rest are great,

00:45:29.751 --> 00:45:32.170
but they're sometimes
misapplied or misunderstood by

00:45:32.171 --> 00:45:35.870
engineers and it leads
them into a morass.

00:45:35.871 --> 00:45:38.620
So, as I mentioned
ML's big impact to

00:45:38.621 --> 00:45:40.050
me has been how it changes

00:45:40.051 --> 00:45:41.955
Software Engineering
and deployment.

00:45:41.956 --> 00:45:45.000
It's introduced this new JVM
layer and has allowed people

00:45:45.001 --> 00:45:48.695
to programme far more easily
than they could before.

00:45:48.696 --> 00:45:52.090
But a lot of what engineers
really want is just not to

00:45:52.091 --> 00:45:54.960
have to tediously specify
every line of their program.

00:45:54.961 --> 00:45:57.010
They really want something
that looks a lot more like

00:45:57.011 --> 00:45:58.560
interpolation and so we actually

00:45:58.561 --> 00:46:00.515
started thinking about
this a while back about,

00:46:00.516 --> 00:46:02.775
well what would an interpolation
system look like?

00:46:02.776 --> 00:46:04.150
Now conventionally,
if you talk to

00:46:04.151 --> 00:46:05.920
an ML person which
is not formally

00:46:05.921 --> 00:46:07.470
true for any reason
that the inequality

00:46:07.471 --> 00:46:09.260
goes the wrong direction
to make this statement,

00:46:09.261 --> 00:46:11.800
they think that an interpolation
implies that you're not

00:46:11.801 --> 00:46:13.350
learning because
interpolation means

00:46:13.351 --> 00:46:14.905
you're memorizing
the training set,

00:46:14.906 --> 00:46:16.525
you're getting zero loss.

00:46:16.526 --> 00:46:19.425
But these two things are
not actually in opposition.

00:46:19.426 --> 00:46:21.670
You can actually get
robust interpolation,

00:46:21.671 --> 00:46:22.900
you can get everything right,

00:46:22.901 --> 00:46:24.790
you can do what
you're told and still

00:46:24.791 --> 00:46:27.215
learn and generalize to
the rest of the dataset.

00:46:27.216 --> 00:46:28.770
This is a pretty
interesting thing

00:46:28.771 --> 00:46:31.115
because traditionally when
I watch engineer struggle,

00:46:31.116 --> 00:46:33.765
they'll say things like
the model is overfitting,

00:46:33.766 --> 00:46:36.120
but they'll have no idea what
they're talking about is

00:46:36.121 --> 00:46:37.540
the error that's
on their test set

00:46:37.541 --> 00:46:38.750
to their validation set.

00:46:38.751 --> 00:46:40.820
It has nothing to do with
over fitting, but they sort

00:46:40.821 --> 00:46:43.200
of adopt this ethos
that learning basically

00:46:43.201 --> 00:46:44.985
means you have to make mistakes

00:46:44.986 --> 00:46:47.650
and that's not true on
a lot of the products.

00:46:47.651 --> 00:46:49.580
If 100 people ask
a question every day,

00:46:49.581 --> 00:46:51.785
every assistant in the world
should get it right.

00:46:51.786 --> 00:46:53.240
That doesn't actually happen.

00:46:53.241 --> 00:46:55.430
So, I think
reliable interpolation

00:46:55.431 --> 00:46:56.910
is one way to think about sort of

00:46:56.911 --> 00:46:57.990
a next generation for

00:46:57.991 --> 00:47:00.715
our learning and statistical
ideas can really play well.

00:47:00.716 --> 00:47:02.420
Now, when I was thinking
about this I thought it

00:47:02.421 --> 00:47:04.340
myself it was kind of heresy
but then I went

00:47:04.341 --> 00:47:06.080
to ISMP which is
this great conferences

00:47:06.081 --> 00:47:08.040
mathematical programming
conference and I was lucky

00:47:08.041 --> 00:47:09.725
enough that Misha was
speaking after me

00:47:09.726 --> 00:47:12.270
and as a result I was
reading some of his papers.

00:47:12.271 --> 00:47:14.480
Misha stays at Ohio State, you
should check out his work.

00:47:14.481 --> 00:47:15.800
He had this really cool stuff

00:47:15.801 --> 00:47:17.410
which is a little bit
insider baseball,

00:47:17.411 --> 00:47:19.460
but it's pretty
interesting which is about

00:47:19.461 --> 00:47:22.830
how the kernel methods
can actually interpolate,

00:47:22.831 --> 00:47:25.070
a family like interpolate
people knew that part

00:47:25.071 --> 00:47:27.610
but could also get
minimax optimal rates.

00:47:27.611 --> 00:47:29.720
So, like the best rates
you could hope for

00:47:29.721 --> 00:47:31.770
these nonparametric
estimator is known

00:47:31.771 --> 00:47:34.500
of a corresponding holder class.

00:47:34.501 --> 00:47:36.555
This is really fascinating

00:47:36.556 --> 00:47:38.395
because it means that
they're not on opposition,

00:47:38.396 --> 00:47:39.630
so we have a chance of building

00:47:39.631 --> 00:47:41.690
dramatically more
reliable systems than we

00:47:41.691 --> 00:47:43.845
were building before
potentially, right.

00:47:43.846 --> 00:47:45.665
So, that's an interesting way
to think about it.

00:47:45.666 --> 00:47:47.960
Also, those minimax rates
are all up to

00:47:47.961 --> 00:47:50.565
label noise and so as I
was talking about before,

00:47:50.566 --> 00:47:52.060
the role of why we're modeling

00:47:52.061 --> 00:47:54.225
label noise in
our construction process,

00:47:54.226 --> 00:47:55.470
you could potentially get better

00:47:55.471 --> 00:47:56.720
rates by modeling that noise and

00:47:56.721 --> 00:47:59.110
that's a program that we're
carrying out right now.

00:47:59.111 --> 00:48:01.505
If you haven't run
Misha's stuff, it's amazing.

00:48:01.506 --> 00:48:03.270
His work is really one of

00:48:03.271 --> 00:48:04.410
those fun times of being in

00:48:04.411 --> 00:48:05.770
academia where you read someone

00:48:05.771 --> 00:48:06.890
else's papers and send them like

00:48:06.891 --> 00:48:08.560
frantic happy emails about how

00:48:08.561 --> 00:48:09.950
exciting it was and I

00:48:09.951 --> 00:48:11.900
got that chance to go hang
out with him in Bordeaux.

00:48:11.901 --> 00:48:14.325
Definitely check his stuff
out I think it's super cool.

00:48:14.326 --> 00:48:16.565
So, that's the end of my talk,

00:48:16.566 --> 00:48:18.520
it's at the end of
the reality distortion bubble,

00:48:18.521 --> 00:48:20.150
I don't know, but
what I wanted to get

00:48:20.151 --> 00:48:21.990
across to you if you don't
know who the dog is,

00:48:21.991 --> 00:48:23.440
you haven't been
reading enough reddit.

00:48:23.441 --> 00:48:26.240
Anyway, but what I
want to get across

00:48:26.241 --> 00:48:27.590
to you is that I
think programming

00:48:27.591 --> 00:48:29.250
is really fundamentally changing,

00:48:29.251 --> 00:48:30.870
its changing from
below in terms of

00:48:30.871 --> 00:48:32.920
how we actually build
our applications and

00:48:32.921 --> 00:48:34.590
this opportunity for
hardware to rise

00:48:34.591 --> 00:48:36.620
up and meet this new
abstraction where there are

00:48:36.621 --> 00:48:39.290
huge number of high dollar
workloads that are going into

00:48:39.291 --> 00:48:41.499
a fairly narrow but still

00:48:41.500 --> 00:48:44.070
kind of general pipeline,
that's really exciting.

00:48:44.071 --> 00:48:46.875
I talked a little bit just as
proof of concept that hey,

00:48:46.876 --> 00:48:48.660
there's more stuff
we can model and

00:48:48.661 --> 00:48:51.605
the process that generates
the data for machine learning.

00:48:51.606 --> 00:48:52.750
That's all Snorkel is really

00:48:52.751 --> 00:48:54.400
about and the auto augment staff.

00:48:54.401 --> 00:48:57.090
All that staff is really
about what goes into the top,

00:48:57.091 --> 00:48:58.250
can we model more of that

00:48:58.251 --> 00:49:00.040
instead of having
it as a black box,

00:49:00.041 --> 00:49:01.570
what are those models look like?

00:49:01.571 --> 00:49:02.855
We showed a first cut,

00:49:02.856 --> 00:49:04.130
but there's a lot more to do.

00:49:04.131 --> 00:49:06.660
Thanks so much for
your time and attention.

00:49:11.050 --> 00:49:13.630
&gt;&gt; I forgot to
mention that I'm sure

00:49:13.631 --> 00:49:15.250
all of us have
a bunch of questions,

00:49:15.251 --> 00:49:17.290
we're just going to do our Q

00:49:17.291 --> 00:49:18.400
and A session at the end with all

00:49:18.401 --> 00:49:19.620
the four speakers up here.

00:49:19.621 --> 00:49:23.630
So, yeah please hold
on to your questions.

00:49:23.631 --> 00:49:27.055
So, our next speaker
is Vivian C. Vivian is

00:49:27.056 --> 00:49:31.050
an associate professor
at MIT in the

00:49:31.051 --> 00:49:33.125
Electrical Engineering and
Computer Science department.

00:49:33.126 --> 00:49:35.740
She's really one of
the world's leading experts

00:49:35.741 --> 00:49:38.260
in energy of signal
processing algorithms and

00:49:38.261 --> 00:49:40.195
more recently has been looking at

00:49:40.196 --> 00:49:43.870
A6 for DNNs and machine
learning algorithms.

00:49:43.871 --> 00:49:46.870
Prior to joining MIT,
she was a member of

00:49:46.871 --> 00:49:49.860
technical staff in RND Center
at Texas Instruments,

00:49:49.861 --> 00:49:52.860
where she was a part
of the GCT video

00:49:52.861 --> 00:49:56.250
coding committee in defining

00:49:56.251 --> 00:49:59.700
the high efficiency
video coding standard

00:49:59.701 --> 00:50:00.910
and for this she received

00:50:00.911 --> 00:50:02.850
the primetime Emmy
engineering Awards.

00:50:02.851 --> 00:50:04.670
So, she's raising the bar.

00:50:04.671 --> 00:50:07.420
She's raising the bar really
high for these awards.

00:50:07.421 --> 00:50:10.790
Emmy's and Oscars and Oscar next.

00:50:10.791 --> 00:50:12.735
&gt;&gt; Okay, sounds good.

00:50:12.736 --> 00:50:14.810
Anyway thank you very much
for the introduction.

00:50:14.811 --> 00:50:16.270
So, the focus of my talk will

00:50:16.271 --> 00:50:17.670
be a little bit different
primarily going to

00:50:17.671 --> 00:50:19.340
focus on how do we enable

00:50:19.341 --> 00:50:21.550
energy-efficient
computing at the edge

00:50:21.551 --> 00:50:23.675
for both deep learnings
and beyond that.

00:50:23.676 --> 00:50:25.630
This work is in
collaboration with a lot

00:50:25.631 --> 00:50:28.855
of my students as well as
other collaborators at MIT.

00:50:28.856 --> 00:50:30.550
So, I'm going to
try and cover three

00:50:30.551 --> 00:50:32.530
key messages in my talks.

00:50:32.531 --> 00:50:33.000
The first one, we
are going to talk

00:50:33.001 --> 00:50:34.160
about the limitations of

00:50:34.161 --> 00:50:36.665
existing efficient DNN
design approaches,

00:50:36.666 --> 00:50:38.875
then we're going to talk
about looking beyond.

00:50:38.876 --> 00:50:41.160
We'll talk a little about DNN
accelerators but can we get

00:50:41.161 --> 00:50:43.985
acceleration by looking beyond
the accelerator itself,

00:50:43.986 --> 00:50:46.520
and then looking at
other opportunities of

00:50:46.521 --> 00:50:49.755
doing inference at the edge
beyond DNNs in general.

00:50:49.756 --> 00:50:51.940
So, very briefly, what
are the limitations

00:50:51.941 --> 00:50:53.530
of existing DNN approaches?

00:50:53.531 --> 00:50:55.300
So, we know that in over

00:50:55.301 --> 00:50:56.690
the past couple of
years there's been

00:50:56.691 --> 00:50:58.630
a huge amount of effort to

00:50:58.631 --> 00:51:00.210
enable energy
efficient processing

00:51:00.211 --> 00:51:01.610
of deep neural nets primarily

00:51:01.611 --> 00:51:05.115
because it's very desirable
on the edge devices.

00:51:05.116 --> 00:51:06.470
So, within my team,

00:51:06.471 --> 00:51:07.490
we've been spending a lot of time

00:51:07.491 --> 00:51:08.840
building tutorial material and

00:51:08.841 --> 00:51:11.540
surveys about this topic and

00:51:11.541 --> 00:51:12.670
through this process we actually

00:51:12.671 --> 00:51:14.160
found there were
several limitations in

00:51:14.161 --> 00:51:15.780
the existing approaches
that I want to

00:51:15.781 --> 00:51:17.875
highlight for
the research community.

00:51:17.876 --> 00:51:20.100
So, the first thing
is that because

00:51:20.101 --> 00:51:20.940
there's been a huge amount of

00:51:20.941 --> 00:51:22.140
effort both on the algorithm,

00:51:22.141 --> 00:51:24.315
the hardware side particularly
in algorithm side,

00:51:24.316 --> 00:51:25.750
we've seen things like network

00:51:25.751 --> 00:51:27.945
pruning where you make
the network sparse.

00:51:27.946 --> 00:51:30.320
A lot of different neural
network architectures to make

00:51:30.321 --> 00:51:32.935
it more compact and
also reduce precision.

00:51:32.936 --> 00:51:35.420
In this particular set of work,

00:51:35.421 --> 00:51:37.460
primarily the focus has
always been on reducing

00:51:37.461 --> 00:51:39.980
the number of knots and
multiplies and accumulate.

00:51:39.981 --> 00:51:42.485
So, computation as
well as weights,

00:51:42.486 --> 00:51:44.440
but one question that we
really have and we're

00:51:44.441 --> 00:51:46.040
looking at the edge is

00:51:46.041 --> 00:51:47.750
does this translate
into energy savings

00:51:47.751 --> 00:51:49.770
because that's what
really directly matters.

00:51:49.771 --> 00:51:51.390
I mean we do know that we

00:51:51.391 --> 00:51:53.510
look at the computer architecture

00:51:53.511 --> 00:51:55.600
itself or where the energy

00:51:55.601 --> 00:51:58.055
actually goes and mostly
goes into data movement.

00:51:58.056 --> 00:52:00.030
Primarily, through
the memory hierarchy.

00:52:00.031 --> 00:52:01.925
So, really what matters is

00:52:01.926 --> 00:52:04.850
the order by which the weight
or the data that goes to

00:52:04.851 --> 00:52:05.980
the neural network passes

00:52:05.981 --> 00:52:06.990
through this memory hierarchy and

00:52:06.991 --> 00:52:10.450
the data flow of
the overall movement.

00:52:10.451 --> 00:52:12.850
So, in some sense you can
say that the weights and

00:52:12.851 --> 00:52:15.090
the operations are
not all in all equal,

00:52:15.091 --> 00:52:17.480
it really matters how they
move through the system.

00:52:17.481 --> 00:52:19.000
To model this some of

00:52:19.001 --> 00:52:20.940
the recent research within
my group has been to

00:52:20.941 --> 00:52:24.520
build up a modeling methodology
to model data movements.

00:52:24.521 --> 00:52:26.470
So for example, here
this is a model that's

00:52:26.471 --> 00:52:28.720
based on our iris accelerator

00:52:28.721 --> 00:52:30.800
where you can take in
various DNN shapes

00:52:30.801 --> 00:52:33.190
as well as the sparsity of
the inputs and the weights.

00:52:33.191 --> 00:52:34.480
You can get a breakdown in

00:52:34.481 --> 00:52:35.820
terms of where
the energy is actually

00:52:35.821 --> 00:52:39.520
going from layer to layer and
where in the actual system.

00:52:39.521 --> 00:52:42.000
So, using information like this,

00:52:42.001 --> 00:52:43.760
one thing that we first
want to figure out

00:52:43.761 --> 00:52:45.440
is where is the
actually energy going

00:52:45.441 --> 00:52:46.600
and what is the first
thing I want to

00:52:46.601 --> 00:52:48.050
really highlight is
that the number of

00:52:48.051 --> 00:52:49.400
weights alone is not necessarily

00:52:49.401 --> 00:52:51.210
good metric of the energy itself.

00:52:51.211 --> 00:52:53.620
There's many different types
of data movements that go

00:52:53.621 --> 00:52:56.100
on within the system itself,
many different data types.

00:52:56.101 --> 00:52:58.535
For example, if we look at
Google and we know that

00:52:58.536 --> 00:53:01.000
weights account for
maybe 22% of the energy

00:53:01.001 --> 00:53:02.650
from data movement but we can see

00:53:02.651 --> 00:53:04.390
that also feature
maps with input and

00:53:04.391 --> 00:53:06.720
output also affect or
significantly affect

00:53:06.721 --> 00:53:09.435
the energy of the system as
well through data movement.

00:53:09.436 --> 00:53:11.705
So, we really shouldn't
just be focusing on

00:53:11.706 --> 00:53:13.680
the number of weights
in terms of trying to

00:53:13.681 --> 00:53:16.395
estimate the energy cost.

00:53:16.396 --> 00:53:18.960
And so, one of the thing
that I really want to

00:53:18.961 --> 00:53:20.990
emphasize or push
is that the idea is

00:53:20.991 --> 00:53:23.140
that we really what we want
to do is rather than taking

00:53:23.141 --> 00:53:24.270
weights or number of

00:53:24.271 --> 00:53:26.320
operations as a metric
that we want to reduce.

00:53:26.321 --> 00:53:27.430
So, they're really easy to

00:53:27.431 --> 00:53:29.890
calculate by looking at
the neural network itself.

00:53:29.891 --> 00:53:32.330
If we really care about
energy or latency we

00:53:32.331 --> 00:53:34.965
should directly target
those particular metrics.

00:53:34.966 --> 00:53:37.550
During the design of
the neural network itself.

00:53:37.551 --> 00:53:39.320
So, we can show for example,

00:53:39.321 --> 00:53:41.785
one example of this is the
energy aware pruning approach.

00:53:41.786 --> 00:53:45.030
The idea here is that if
we the layers based on

00:53:45.031 --> 00:53:47.160
the energy consumption
and let's say we start

00:53:47.161 --> 00:53:49.380
pruning the layers that
consume the most energy first,

00:53:49.381 --> 00:53:50.845
we know that as you prune more,

00:53:50.846 --> 00:53:52.385
accuracy tends to go down.

00:53:52.386 --> 00:53:53.995
So, you basically want to spend,

00:53:53.996 --> 00:53:55.360
get the best bank for your buck,

00:53:55.361 --> 00:53:56.600
spend the money where,

00:53:56.601 --> 00:53:58.370
spend the pruning where
it matters the most.

00:53:58.371 --> 00:54:00.680
If you do that as
opposed to just removing

00:54:00.681 --> 00:54:03.585
weights that tend to be small,

00:54:03.586 --> 00:54:05.270
you actually get
better energy savings.

00:54:05.271 --> 00:54:07.330
So, compared to
a typical magnitude based

00:54:07.331 --> 00:54:09.940
pruning approach which is
actually quite effective,

00:54:09.941 --> 00:54:12.140
you can do even better with
energy where pruning by

00:54:12.141 --> 00:54:14.400
really removing the weights
where they actually matters.

00:54:14.401 --> 00:54:16.140
So, we can see here
for example on

00:54:16.141 --> 00:54:18.550
AlexNet about a 1.7 x
reduction in energy,

00:54:18.551 --> 00:54:21.310
by driving really
the algorithm design

00:54:21.311 --> 00:54:23.940
based with the energy metrics

00:54:23.941 --> 00:54:26.685
as opposed to just looking
at number of weights.

00:54:26.686 --> 00:54:29.390
One challenge of course
is that what if you don't

00:54:29.391 --> 00:54:31.820
actually have an energy model
available for you,

00:54:31.821 --> 00:54:34.290
can you automate this whole
process by actually taking

00:54:34.291 --> 00:54:37.810
direct metrics from
an actual platform itself?

00:54:37.811 --> 00:54:40.010
So, this is some work that
we've done recently with

00:54:40.011 --> 00:54:41.960
Google's mobile Vision Team
and the idea here

00:54:41.961 --> 00:54:44.020
for net adapt, is
that rather than.

00:54:44.021 --> 00:54:45.930
Again, using an energy
model to estimate

00:54:45.931 --> 00:54:48.380
the energy or the latency,

00:54:48.381 --> 00:54:49.800
we can take direct measurements

00:54:49.801 --> 00:54:51.445
from a mobile platform itself.

00:54:51.446 --> 00:54:53.230
This will incorporate things like

00:54:53.231 --> 00:54:55.060
the effects of the tool chain,

00:54:55.061 --> 00:54:56.820
and if you have some
proprietary hardware where

00:54:56.821 --> 00:54:58.790
you don't actually have
the low-level details of,

00:54:58.791 --> 00:55:00.530
you can still take
that information,

00:55:00.531 --> 00:55:02.250
those metrics that
you care about and

00:55:02.251 --> 00:55:04.100
insert it into
the net adapt algorithm.

00:55:04.101 --> 00:55:07.095
And automatically adapt
the algorithm to really minimize

00:55:07.096 --> 00:55:10.990
either latency or energy
or a combination of both.

00:55:10.991 --> 00:55:12.460
These are much more directed

00:55:12.461 --> 00:55:15.245
towards the metrics that
you actually care about

00:55:15.246 --> 00:55:17.180
when optimizing
the system as opposed to

00:55:17.181 --> 00:55:19.040
again just counting the weights

00:55:19.041 --> 00:55:20.725
or counting number of apps.

00:55:20.726 --> 00:55:23.110
To just show a case
the impact of this,

00:55:23.111 --> 00:55:24.780
if you look at
the trade-off in terms of

00:55:24.781 --> 00:55:26.680
let's say here accuracy
versus latency.

00:55:26.681 --> 00:55:28.830
So, the top left is better,

00:55:28.831 --> 00:55:30.870
you can see this is net adapt

00:55:30.871 --> 00:55:32.800
applied to mobile net
and this is actually

00:55:32.801 --> 00:55:34.070
the smallest version of

00:55:34.071 --> 00:55:35.280
a very small version of mobile

00:55:35.281 --> 00:55:36.680
that which is more
difficult to prune.

00:55:36.681 --> 00:55:39.740
You can see is that you
can get up to about 1.7

00:55:39.741 --> 00:55:43.315
x reduction in
latency for the same,

00:55:43.316 --> 00:55:44.835
around this same accuracy.

00:55:44.836 --> 00:55:46.300
This is really because
you're driving

00:55:46.301 --> 00:55:47.440
the algorithm based on

00:55:47.441 --> 00:55:48.900
latency as opposed
to that based on

00:55:48.901 --> 00:55:50.735
reducing the number
of operations.

00:55:50.736 --> 00:55:52.010
So, again the message here is,

00:55:52.011 --> 00:55:53.870
really can you incorporate

00:55:53.871 --> 00:55:56.360
the direct metrics that
you care about latency,

00:55:56.361 --> 00:55:58.640
energy or whatever
your system metrics

00:55:58.641 --> 00:56:01.120
are directly into
the optimization of

00:56:01.121 --> 00:56:03.750
the DNN itself as
opposed to other metrics

00:56:03.751 --> 00:56:07.455
like compute or
number of weights.

00:56:07.456 --> 00:56:09.860
Another thing I just want to
highlight more looking at

00:56:09.861 --> 00:56:11.450
the hardware side is
that now that we have

00:56:11.451 --> 00:56:14.000
many of these energy-efficient
DNN design approaches

00:56:14.001 --> 00:56:15.590
from the algorithmic perspective,

00:56:15.591 --> 00:56:17.820
the one challenge from
the hardware perspective

00:56:17.821 --> 00:56:19.055
and when you build accelerators,

00:56:19.056 --> 00:56:22.260
is that there is no guarantee
as to which DNN approach or

00:56:22.261 --> 00:56:24.240
algorithm and algorithm designer

00:56:24.241 --> 00:56:26.575
will actually use and try and
launch onto your hardware.

00:56:26.576 --> 00:56:28.200
So, what's really
important also is to

00:56:28.201 --> 00:56:29.780
be able to develop
hardware that's

00:56:29.781 --> 00:56:31.290
flexible enough to exploit

00:56:31.291 --> 00:56:34.010
these different features
of these DNN algorithms.

00:56:34.011 --> 00:56:36.080
So, one of the challenges that we

00:56:36.081 --> 00:56:37.720
see from doing this
is particularly

00:56:37.721 --> 00:56:39.040
in the design of specialized DNN

00:56:39.041 --> 00:56:40.630
hardware and they often rely

00:56:40.631 --> 00:56:42.170
on certain properties
of the DNN to

00:56:42.171 --> 00:56:43.870
achieve the high
energy efficiency.

00:56:43.871 --> 00:56:46.620
So, very typical
specialized DNN hardware

00:56:46.621 --> 00:56:49.565
will use an array of
multiplies and accumulates.

00:56:49.566 --> 00:56:52.300
One benefit of doing that
is that you can reduce

00:56:52.301 --> 00:56:53.490
the memory access by

00:56:53.491 --> 00:56:55.690
amortizing the reads
across the MAC array.

00:56:55.691 --> 00:56:57.730
So, for example, in

00:56:57.731 --> 00:57:00.665
this particular depiction
when I read the weights,

00:57:00.666 --> 00:57:01.910
I can reuse them across

00:57:01.911 --> 00:57:04.165
multiple columns
of the MAC array.

00:57:04.166 --> 00:57:06.460
When I read an activation,

00:57:06.461 --> 00:57:08.840
I can use it across
multiple rows of the array.

00:57:08.841 --> 00:57:10.810
So there's a lot of
reuse here and as we

00:57:10.811 --> 00:57:12.900
mentioned before memory
access is very expensive.

00:57:12.901 --> 00:57:14.010
So, the more you can reuse,

00:57:14.011 --> 00:57:16.140
the more you can reduce
the energy costs

00:57:16.141 --> 00:57:18.135
and make the system
more energy efficient.

00:57:18.136 --> 00:57:20.820
However, as we mentioned a lot of

00:57:20.821 --> 00:57:22.180
these network architectures are

00:57:22.181 --> 00:57:23.935
continuing to evolve and change.

00:57:23.936 --> 00:57:25.650
So, usually the challenge with

00:57:25.651 --> 00:57:26.680
these particular types of

00:57:26.681 --> 00:57:28.300
architectures that we saw before

00:57:28.301 --> 00:57:30.470
was that the reuse and
the reutilization really

00:57:30.471 --> 00:57:33.110
depends on specific
properties of the DNN.

00:57:33.111 --> 00:57:35.020
So, for example, the
number of channels,

00:57:35.021 --> 00:57:36.985
the feature map or batch size.

00:57:36.986 --> 00:57:38.455
So as a result,

00:57:38.456 --> 00:57:40.570
when you build hardware

00:57:40.571 --> 00:57:42.210
that really relies on
certain properties of

00:57:42.211 --> 00:57:44.360
the DNN but then the DNNs

00:57:44.361 --> 00:57:46.565
are varying in terms
of their architecture,

00:57:46.566 --> 00:57:48.370
it's not going to be
enable your DNNs to

00:57:48.371 --> 00:57:50.310
be or your accelerators
to be efficient

00:57:50.311 --> 00:57:54.300
across a wide range of
compact DNN architectures.

00:57:54.301 --> 00:57:56.200
The other thing to also
consider is that we

00:57:56.201 --> 00:57:58.030
often want to scale
up the size of

00:57:58.031 --> 00:58:01.040
these arrays for
your accelerators

00:58:01.041 --> 00:58:02.470
and so it's going to
become less efficient

00:58:02.471 --> 00:58:05.630
if you pin the utilization on

00:58:05.631 --> 00:58:07.380
certain dimensions of
the DNN and then you

00:58:07.381 --> 00:58:09.240
scale up the array size,

00:58:09.241 --> 00:58:11.550
the likelihood is the utilization

00:58:11.551 --> 00:58:13.840
of your array is going
to reduce as well.

00:58:13.841 --> 00:58:15.800
So, another thing is of course we

00:58:15.801 --> 00:58:17.690
mentioned that pruning and
sparcities they're also

00:58:17.691 --> 00:58:21.890
effective method of potentially
reducing the cost of

00:58:21.891 --> 00:58:23.760
processing these DNNs
but this can be

00:58:23.761 --> 00:58:24.760
challenging if
you're going to use

00:58:24.761 --> 00:58:27.100
this pinned array structure.

00:58:27.101 --> 00:58:28.950
So, one of the focuses

00:58:28.951 --> 00:58:30.420
of our next generation
eyeriss is really

00:58:30.421 --> 00:58:33.215
trying to balance
this flexibility and efficiency,

00:58:33.216 --> 00:58:35.210
what we want is to be able
to support a wide range of

00:58:35.211 --> 00:58:38.225
filter shapes with
large and compact.

00:58:38.226 --> 00:58:40.080
Then many different
types of layers,

00:58:40.081 --> 00:58:41.470
both convolutional layers and

00:58:41.471 --> 00:58:42.480
fully connected layers but

00:58:42.481 --> 00:58:43.780
also things if you
start thinking like

00:58:43.781 --> 00:58:45.200
mobile net depth wise layer is

00:58:45.201 --> 00:58:46.820
also important to
support and then

00:58:46.821 --> 00:58:48.430
often tends to be
challenging because

00:58:48.431 --> 00:58:50.925
the number of channels is
significantly reduced.

00:58:50.926 --> 00:58:53.220
You want to be able to
support a wide range of

00:58:53.221 --> 00:58:55.320
sparsity both dense and
sparse since you often

00:58:55.321 --> 00:58:57.775
don't have control as
the hardware designers to

00:58:57.776 --> 00:58:59.500
the degree of sparsity
of the neural network

00:58:59.501 --> 00:59:01.770
that's going to be
deployed on your device,

00:59:01.771 --> 00:59:04.565
and then you also want your
architecture to be scalable.

00:59:04.566 --> 00:59:05.820
So, for example, if I scale up

00:59:05.821 --> 00:59:07.250
the number of
processing elements,

00:59:07.251 --> 00:59:10.035
I also want to see that
my throughput also scales up

00:59:10.036 --> 00:59:13.620
and that my energy efficiency
is also getting better.

00:59:13.621 --> 00:59:16.775
So, what's really important
is that you're able to find,

00:59:16.776 --> 00:59:19.360
like if you have a very small
compact neural network,

00:59:19.361 --> 00:59:21.500
you have the flexibility
to find where

00:59:21.501 --> 00:59:25.315
or what dimension you can reuse
as much data as possible.

00:59:25.316 --> 00:59:29.589
So, by having
a more flexible dataflow

00:59:29.590 --> 00:59:32.835
and NoC and which we'll
talk about next for our C2,

00:59:32.836 --> 00:59:34.100
you're able to get
over an order of

00:59:34.101 --> 00:59:35.800
magnitude savings in terms

00:59:35.801 --> 00:59:37.410
of both speed as well

00:59:37.411 --> 00:59:39.240
as energy efficiency
compared to V1.

00:59:39.241 --> 00:59:41.330
Just to give you an idea
in terms of how we're

00:59:41.331 --> 00:59:43.960
getting this flexibility
and Eyeriss V2,

00:59:43.961 --> 00:59:45.540
primarily the main
focus is to have

00:59:45.541 --> 00:59:48.450
a much more flexible dataflow
and in particular we extend

00:59:48.451 --> 00:59:49.840
Row-Stationary Dataflow into

00:59:49.841 --> 00:59:52.090
Row-Stationary Plus and
what this really means

00:59:52.091 --> 00:59:53.990
is that enables you to
do more spatial mapping

00:59:53.991 --> 00:59:55.740
and all dimensions
of the PE array,

00:59:55.741 --> 00:59:57.285
so that you can exploit

00:59:57.286 --> 01:00:00.540
more data reuse in a multiple
different layer shapes.

01:00:00.541 --> 01:00:02.390
So, for example,
previously if you're

01:00:02.391 --> 01:00:04.440
looking at let's say
a depth wise layer,

01:00:04.441 --> 01:00:06.580
you really have very

01:00:06.581 --> 01:00:08.230
limited in terms of number
of channels you have but

01:00:08.231 --> 01:00:10.100
if you can actually also with

01:00:10.101 --> 01:00:11.555
Row-Stationary Plus not only

01:00:11.556 --> 01:00:13.260
fill up your array
based on filter width

01:00:13.261 --> 01:00:14.600
but also channel
groups and you can

01:00:14.601 --> 01:00:16.240
really utilize your array much

01:00:16.241 --> 01:00:17.850
more fully and then
have higher throughput

01:00:17.851 --> 01:00:19.855
and have higher efficiency.

01:00:19.856 --> 01:00:22.670
To go along with
the more flexible dataflow,

01:00:22.671 --> 01:00:24.950
what you also need is
a more flexible NoC where

01:00:24.951 --> 01:00:26.270
node C to actually support

01:00:26.271 --> 01:00:27.840
all these different
configurations.

01:00:27.841 --> 01:00:31.675
In particular, you want to be
able to have a NoC that you

01:00:31.676 --> 01:00:33.600
can deliver multicasts when you

01:00:33.601 --> 01:00:35.590
ignore to exploit
high data reuse.

01:00:35.591 --> 01:00:37.760
So, for example, if you
have a very large network

01:00:37.761 --> 01:00:40.190
like AlexNet where there's
a lot of reuse opportunity,

01:00:40.191 --> 01:00:41.660
you want to have a
NoC that can find

01:00:41.661 --> 01:00:43.295
that reuse and make
good use of it,

01:00:43.296 --> 01:00:44.790
but at the same time you also

01:00:44.791 --> 01:00:46.100
want this NoC to be flexible in

01:00:46.101 --> 01:00:47.390
the case where you have

01:00:47.391 --> 01:00:48.910
layers that don't
have as much reuse.

01:00:48.911 --> 01:00:51.240
So, for example, the fully
connected layers or the

01:00:51.241 --> 01:00:53.620
depth wise layers in
the compact networks and then in

01:00:53.621 --> 01:00:54.900
that case you want you're NoC to

01:00:54.901 --> 01:00:56.240
be able to do unicasts and

01:00:56.241 --> 01:00:59.975
deliver a high bandwidth
just the same throughput.

01:00:59.976 --> 01:01:01.920
Then of course as I
mentioned, there's

01:01:01.921 --> 01:01:03.070
no real control in terms

01:01:03.071 --> 01:01:05.420
of the degree of
sparsity that you have,

01:01:05.421 --> 01:01:06.620
so you also want
to make sure that

01:01:06.621 --> 01:01:08.780
your hardwares or you're
NoC is flexible off to

01:01:08.781 --> 01:01:11.000
support both sparse
and dense layers

01:01:11.001 --> 01:01:12.620
and both the weights and

01:01:12.621 --> 01:01:15.220
activations for the various
different configurations

01:01:15.221 --> 01:01:16.570
rather it be convolutional layers

01:01:16.571 --> 01:01:18.820
or the fully connected layers.

01:01:18.860 --> 01:01:21.405
So, that's basically
what you can do

01:01:21.406 --> 01:01:23.370
from the DNN accelerator
point of view.

01:01:23.371 --> 01:01:24.770
So, really again
the message is to have

01:01:24.771 --> 01:01:27.100
more flexibility from
your hardware and to really to

01:01:27.101 --> 01:01:29.250
drive the design of
your neural net algorithms

01:01:29.251 --> 01:01:31.805
using real metrics such
as latency energy.

01:01:31.806 --> 01:01:34.205
The one thing though
is sometimes there's

01:01:34.206 --> 01:01:35.360
a certain limit in terms of how

01:01:35.361 --> 01:01:37.310
fast you can actually
make your accelerator.

01:01:37.311 --> 01:01:38.660
So, one thing to do is also look

01:01:38.661 --> 01:01:40.200
beyond the accelerator itself and

01:01:40.201 --> 01:01:43.335
find other opportunities to
accelerate your processing.

01:01:43.336 --> 01:01:45.120
So, I'll give one
example of why it's

01:01:45.121 --> 01:01:47.130
important to look beyond
the DNN accelerator,

01:01:47.131 --> 01:01:48.850
what opportunities
there are particularly

01:01:48.851 --> 01:01:51.050
in the space of super-resolution.

01:01:51.051 --> 01:01:55.670
So, one important
application that

01:01:55.671 --> 01:01:58.410
super-resolution serves on
mobile devices is again taking

01:01:58.411 --> 01:02:00.150
low-resolution images and making

01:02:00.151 --> 01:02:01.515
it to high-resolution image.

01:02:01.516 --> 01:02:03.900
This can be very
useful if you want to

01:02:03.901 --> 01:02:06.340
transmit a low-resolution
image because you

01:02:06.341 --> 01:02:08.480
have very limited bandwidth

01:02:08.481 --> 01:02:09.950
or because just the sizes

01:02:09.951 --> 01:02:11.790
that are screens
continue to grow.

01:02:11.791 --> 01:02:14.705
So, it's very useful for
the viewing experience.

01:02:14.706 --> 01:02:16.280
However, on a mobile device of

01:02:16.281 --> 01:02:18.865
course again you have
very limited compute.

01:02:18.866 --> 01:02:21.355
We know that these days
a state-of-the-art

01:02:21.356 --> 01:02:23.390
super-resolution
algorithms often are CNN

01:02:23.391 --> 01:02:25.450
based and in fact if you were

01:02:25.451 --> 01:02:27.540
run them on very high
resolution images, for example,

01:02:27.541 --> 01:02:29.590
from HD to alter
HD their degree of

01:02:29.591 --> 01:02:30.670
compute for these

01:02:30.671 --> 01:02:31.870
super-resolution
algorithms are going to

01:02:31.871 --> 01:02:34.110
be exceptionally high
and they don't map well

01:02:34.111 --> 01:02:37.065
very much to
these mobile platforms.

01:02:37.066 --> 01:02:38.870
So, the one way that we tried to

01:02:38.871 --> 01:02:40.140
address this is to develop

01:02:40.141 --> 01:02:41.350
a framework that we can use

01:02:41.351 --> 01:02:42.780
to accelerate
the super-resolution,

01:02:42.781 --> 01:02:44.270
we call it FAST and the main

01:02:44.271 --> 01:02:46.150
idea is that you're
basically in search

01:02:46.151 --> 01:02:48.390
different super-resolution
algorithms

01:02:48.391 --> 01:02:49.850
into this framework,

01:02:49.851 --> 01:02:51.640
we also taken the
compressed video and then

01:02:51.641 --> 01:02:53.640
you find opportunities
to accelerate it,

01:02:53.641 --> 01:02:56.350
primarily by exploiting
an interesting property

01:02:56.351 --> 01:02:58.370
of the compressed video itself.

01:02:58.371 --> 01:03:00.390
So typically, if you look from

01:03:00.391 --> 01:03:02.040
a super-resolution point of view

01:03:02.041 --> 01:03:04.400
at a video where you

01:03:04.401 --> 01:03:07.775
actually view it as
a bunch of stacked pixels.

01:03:07.776 --> 01:03:10.420
However, if I was
the person who previously

01:03:10.421 --> 01:03:12.825
worked on computer video coding,

01:03:12.826 --> 01:03:14.965
the way that I typically
look at compressed videos,

01:03:14.966 --> 01:03:17.360
is in terms of
a more description of

01:03:17.361 --> 01:03:18.910
the block structure
or the structures

01:03:18.911 --> 01:03:20.695
of the pixels within
the video itself.

01:03:20.696 --> 01:03:21.770
So, the one way where we get

01:03:21.771 --> 01:03:23.750
really effectively
compressed video

01:03:23.751 --> 01:03:25.110
is to describe the video,

01:03:25.111 --> 01:03:26.470
figure out the
correlations between

01:03:26.471 --> 01:03:28.700
the pixels within
the video itself, right?

01:03:28.701 --> 01:03:30.090
So, in fact, the compressed video

01:03:30.091 --> 01:03:31.805
itself is not
just a set of pixels,

01:03:31.806 --> 01:03:33.010
but a lot of information about

01:03:33.011 --> 01:03:36.560
how different pixels in the
video is actually correlated.

01:03:36.561 --> 01:03:39.120
So, the goal here
is that can we use

01:03:39.121 --> 01:03:40.730
this compressed representation to

01:03:40.731 --> 01:03:43.665
help accelerate
super-resolution itself?

01:03:43.666 --> 01:03:45.470
So, in fact, you can.

01:03:45.471 --> 01:03:47.300
So what you can do is
rather than having

01:03:47.301 --> 01:03:49.275
a bunch of Low-res videos and

01:03:49.276 --> 01:03:51.835
applying super-resolution
to every single frame

01:03:51.836 --> 01:03:53.525
of the low-resolution video.

01:03:53.526 --> 01:03:54.790
What you can actually do,

01:03:54.791 --> 01:03:56.970
is you can apply
super-resolution on only one of

01:03:56.971 --> 01:03:58.360
the frames and then use

01:03:58.361 --> 01:04:00.510
this correlation
information, for example,

01:04:00.511 --> 01:04:03.820
motion vectors to actually
transfer the information from

01:04:03.821 --> 01:04:05.000
one frame to what are

01:04:05.001 --> 01:04:06.040
the high-resolution frames to

01:04:06.041 --> 01:04:07.490
all the other
high-resolution frames.

01:04:07.491 --> 01:04:08.640
You can generate basically all

01:04:08.641 --> 01:04:10.125
the other high-resolution frames

01:04:10.126 --> 01:04:11.210
without having to run

01:04:11.211 --> 01:04:12.995
super-resolution on
every single one.

01:04:12.996 --> 01:04:14.690
So, as a result
super-resolution is only

01:04:14.691 --> 01:04:17.885
operating on a subset of
these particular frames.

01:04:17.886 --> 01:04:20.350
This tends to be much more

01:04:20.351 --> 01:04:21.810
computationally efficient

01:04:21.811 --> 01:04:23.310
because this transfer
is very cheap,

01:04:23.311 --> 01:04:24.750
it only involves basically some

01:04:24.751 --> 01:04:26.850
bicubic interpolation
and again it

01:04:26.851 --> 01:04:28.910
involves using
free information that you

01:04:28.911 --> 01:04:31.375
already have in
the compressed video itself.

01:04:31.376 --> 01:04:34.040
So, if you transfer from N frames

01:04:34.041 --> 01:04:37.590
you'll just basically
get an Nx acceleration.

01:04:37.591 --> 01:04:41.320
So, we verified
this particular approach on

01:04:41.321 --> 01:04:43.320
why dataset primarily the

01:04:43.321 --> 01:04:46.035
HEVC development dataset
of 20 different videos.

01:04:46.036 --> 01:04:47.430
We're able to show that if you

01:04:47.431 --> 01:04:48.790
transfer up to four frames,

01:04:48.791 --> 01:04:51.020
is actually no loss
in terms of quality.

01:04:51.021 --> 01:04:52.710
If you transfer up to 16 frames,

01:04:52.711 --> 01:04:54.570
there's about a point two dB drop

01:04:54.571 --> 01:04:57.580
in PSNR which is
not too bad at all.

01:04:57.690 --> 01:05:00.929
Typically for these particular
types of applications,

01:05:00.930 --> 01:05:02.920
it's really important
to actually look at

01:05:02.921 --> 01:05:05.020
the visual quality itself.

01:05:05.021 --> 01:05:06.920
So, shown here in the video are

01:05:06.921 --> 01:05:09.740
three different renditions
of the super-resolution.

01:05:09.741 --> 01:05:11.720
So, on the left-hand
side you have

01:05:11.721 --> 01:05:15.300
the original SRCNN algorithm
with the highest quality.

01:05:15.301 --> 01:05:16.990
On the right-hand side you have

01:05:16.991 --> 01:05:19.460
the bicubic interpolation
and you can see by looking

01:05:19.461 --> 01:05:21.650
at the text of
the cards that it's

01:05:21.651 --> 01:05:24.170
much more fuzzier than the SRCNN.

01:05:24.171 --> 01:05:26.230
However, then you have fast
in the middle which has

01:05:26.231 --> 01:05:27.700
the same complexity as

01:05:27.701 --> 01:05:30.380
the bicubic algorithm
on the right-hand side,

01:05:30.381 --> 01:05:32.890
but you can see that
the quality of fast compared to

01:05:32.891 --> 01:05:36.145
the left-hand side is
also very competitive.

01:05:36.146 --> 01:05:39.330
So, the main idea here is
that while we can do a lot of

01:05:39.331 --> 01:05:40.820
things to really accelerate and

01:05:40.821 --> 01:05:42.955
make the DNN accelerator
more efficient,

01:05:42.956 --> 01:05:44.560
there are other properties
that we can look

01:05:44.561 --> 01:05:46.310
at beyond the accelerator

01:05:46.311 --> 01:05:48.820
itself to improve the processing

01:05:48.821 --> 01:05:49.960
or make the processing
more efficient.

01:05:49.961 --> 01:05:51.110
In this particular case,

01:05:51.111 --> 01:05:54.015
looking at much more
efficient representations

01:05:54.016 --> 01:05:55.340
of the incoming data,

01:05:55.341 --> 01:05:57.765
that's actually there for free.

01:05:57.766 --> 01:06:00.110
So, one final thing is looking

01:06:00.111 --> 01:06:01.985
Beyond Deep Neural
Networks itself.

01:06:01.986 --> 01:06:03.409
On what are some opportunities

01:06:03.410 --> 01:06:04.740
in terms of whether
the types of processing

01:06:04.741 --> 01:06:07.820
that we care about at
the particular edge.

01:06:07.821 --> 01:06:09.360
So, one of the
projects that we've

01:06:09.361 --> 01:06:10.490
been working on within my group,

01:06:10.491 --> 01:06:12.400
this is in collaboration
with Sertac Karamani

01:06:12.401 --> 01:06:14.605
and Luca Carlone in
the AeroAstro department,

01:06:14.606 --> 01:06:17.980
is how to build very low
power processing engines

01:06:17.981 --> 01:06:20.290
for autonomous Drone navigation.

01:06:20.291 --> 01:06:22.540
Particularly, Nanodrones, where
you talking about drones,

01:06:22.541 --> 01:06:24.960
the size of bottle caps
or the size of a quarter.

01:06:24.961 --> 01:06:26.740
So, in this particular project we

01:06:26.741 --> 01:06:28.550
built a chip we call Navion,

01:06:28.551 --> 01:06:30.550
that primarily does localization

01:06:30.551 --> 01:06:32.440
and mapping at under around 30mW.

01:06:32.441 --> 01:06:34.690
The real challenge is really

01:06:34.691 --> 01:06:37.250
fully integrating all of
that processing on chip.

01:06:37.251 --> 01:06:38.350
Because when you're
talking about such a

01:06:38.351 --> 01:06:39.865
small Drone you can't have

01:06:39.866 --> 01:06:43.590
so many external parts
or external components.

01:06:43.591 --> 01:06:46.090
In terms of what
this Drone actually does,

01:06:46.091 --> 01:06:48.460
it actually is performing
Visual-Inertial Odometry,

01:06:48.461 --> 01:06:50.600
is basically taking
in an image sequence

01:06:50.601 --> 01:06:53.040
as well as IMU measurements from

01:06:53.041 --> 01:06:56.325
Inertial accelerometer
plus a gyroscope

01:06:56.326 --> 01:06:58.470
as taking these two
particular inputs in,

01:06:58.471 --> 01:07:00.320
and then try to determine one,

01:07:00.321 --> 01:07:02.270
the location of the drone itself.

01:07:02.271 --> 01:07:03.310
So, shown on the upper right

01:07:03.311 --> 01:07:04.780
hand corner you can
see that based on

01:07:04.781 --> 01:07:06.010
the visual data as well as

01:07:06.011 --> 01:07:08.350
some IMU data is trying
to determine itself,

01:07:08.351 --> 01:07:10.280
in the 3D world as well as

01:07:10.281 --> 01:07:12.160
mapping the environment itself.

01:07:12.161 --> 01:07:13.300
So, that's one of the key goals

01:07:13.301 --> 01:07:16.620
for this particular type
of processing.

01:07:16.621 --> 01:07:19.145
Really one important aspect

01:07:19.146 --> 01:07:20.980
of this particular processing
has to take in,

01:07:20.981 --> 01:07:24.750
as I mentioned the vision input
as well as the IMU input,

01:07:24.751 --> 01:07:27.210
and you want to do some kind
of optimization of

01:07:27.211 --> 01:07:28.550
the vision data as well as

01:07:28.551 --> 01:07:31.000
the estimated state
from the IMU data.

01:07:31.001 --> 01:07:32.830
Typically, this optimization is

01:07:32.831 --> 01:07:34.350
done in the form
of a factor graph,

01:07:34.351 --> 01:07:36.360
to help you infer the location of

01:07:36.361 --> 01:07:39.340
the Drone itself based on
this incoming information.

01:07:39.341 --> 01:07:40.990
Basically, this involves solving

01:07:40.991 --> 01:07:43.590
a nonlinear least square
factor graph optimization.

01:07:43.591 --> 01:07:44.880
You have factors that are

01:07:44.881 --> 01:07:47.120
dictated based on the feature
tracks that you see from

01:07:47.121 --> 01:07:49.325
your vision side as well as

01:07:49.326 --> 01:07:52.270
any IMU measurements
that are coming in.

01:07:52.271 --> 01:07:53.820
Basically, this is a large graph,

01:07:53.821 --> 01:07:56.010
you're talking about
over 4,000 factors,

01:07:56.011 --> 01:07:58.820
all where you're trying to
put this under a small drone.

01:07:58.821 --> 01:08:00.440
Based on these optimizations,

01:08:00.441 --> 01:08:01.860
you can kind of update
what the states

01:08:01.861 --> 01:08:04.330
are and figure out
what the 3D Map is.

01:08:04.331 --> 01:08:07.120
So, very similar to
the deep learning space,

01:08:07.121 --> 01:08:09.295
you can also find
opportunities to exploit

01:08:09.296 --> 01:08:11.870
things like sparsity to
reduce the computation.

01:08:11.871 --> 01:08:14.420
In particular here, we look
at the fact they can exploit

01:08:14.421 --> 01:08:17.340
sparsity you get 5x
reduction in memory,

01:08:17.341 --> 01:08:19.950
which is very important
to the area cost of

01:08:19.951 --> 01:08:21.770
these particular
devices and about

01:08:21.771 --> 01:08:24.280
a 7x increase in
terms of the speed.

01:08:24.281 --> 01:08:25.825
So, what I want to
show here is there are

01:08:25.826 --> 01:08:27.740
other opportunities
other than DNN itself,

01:08:27.741 --> 01:08:29.660
where acceleration
and the co-design

01:08:29.661 --> 01:08:30.700
of both the algorithm and

01:08:30.701 --> 01:08:31.860
the hardware can really

01:08:31.861 --> 01:08:34.670
enable increased
energy efficiency.

01:08:34.671 --> 01:08:36.810
So, to summarize, we talked about

01:08:36.811 --> 01:08:38.440
the various different
design considerations

01:08:38.441 --> 01:08:39.845
for deep learning at the edge.

01:08:39.846 --> 01:08:41.050
The important thing
is to make sure you

01:08:41.051 --> 01:08:42.670
incorporate direct
metrics such as

01:08:42.671 --> 01:08:44.480
latency or energy
into the design of

01:08:44.481 --> 01:08:46.730
the algorithm itself
to improve efficiency.

01:08:46.731 --> 01:08:49.740
It's also really important
given the large number

01:08:49.741 --> 01:08:52.790
of efficient DNN algorithm
techniques out there to

01:08:52.791 --> 01:08:54.700
support hardware that
can really support

01:08:54.701 --> 01:08:57.055
flexible data flows
and NoC to exploit

01:08:57.056 --> 01:08:59.450
both the data reuse for
energy efficiency as well as to

01:08:59.451 --> 01:09:02.875
ensure that your array is
fully utilized for speed.

01:09:02.876 --> 01:09:05.085
It's also important
to look beyond

01:09:05.086 --> 01:09:07.005
the deep learning acceleration
engine itself for

01:09:07.006 --> 01:09:09.730
other opportunities to
accelerate your algorithms,

01:09:09.731 --> 01:09:11.020
in particular you can look at

01:09:11.021 --> 01:09:14.660
the representation of the data
that's actually coming in,

01:09:14.661 --> 01:09:16.010
in this case super-resolution,

01:09:16.011 --> 01:09:17.690
we showed that that
was quite effective.

01:09:17.691 --> 01:09:20.360
Then finally, there are other
forms of inference that can

01:09:20.361 --> 01:09:24.330
actually process at the
edge beyond deep learning.

01:09:24.331 --> 01:09:26.600
So, we showed one example
of using graphical models

01:09:26.601 --> 01:09:29.475
for localization and
mapping for Nanodrones.

01:09:29.476 --> 01:09:32.090
That concludes my talk. Thanks.

01:09:36.910 --> 01:09:40.900
&gt;&gt; Thanks Vivian. So,
our final speaker for

01:09:40.901 --> 01:09:44.425
this session is Jeff Gordon.

01:09:44.426 --> 01:09:48.180
Jeff is the research director for

01:09:48.181 --> 01:09:50.790
the Microsoft Research
Montreal Lab and also

01:09:50.791 --> 01:09:52.560
a professor of machine learning

01:09:52.561 --> 01:09:55.060
at Carnegie Mellon University.

01:09:55.310 --> 01:09:58.940
Jeff is very well known for

01:09:58.941 --> 01:10:00.670
his work on AI systems that

01:10:00.671 --> 01:10:03.380
capture long-term
thinking such as,

01:10:04.690 --> 01:10:10.340
long-term reasoning, reasoning
ahead to solve problems,

01:10:10.341 --> 01:10:11.870
planning a sequence
of actions and

01:10:11.871 --> 01:10:14.205
inferring unseen properties
from observations.

01:10:14.206 --> 01:10:16.980
He also has a very unique
perspective now as

01:10:16.981 --> 01:10:21.335
the head of Microsoft's
latest AI lab in Montreal.

01:10:21.336 --> 01:10:23.270
&gt;&gt; All right, thank
you very much.

01:10:23.271 --> 01:10:28.770
So, I thought the title

01:10:28.771 --> 01:10:32.390
of the session was chosen
very provocatively so,

01:10:32.391 --> 01:10:35.165
I did my best to respond to it.

01:10:35.166 --> 01:10:37.750
So, free inference
and instant training,

01:10:37.751 --> 01:10:40.220
I'm not going to be able
to achieve either of

01:10:40.221 --> 01:10:42.690
those but I wanted to talk a

01:10:42.691 --> 01:10:44.220
little bit about how to

01:10:44.221 --> 01:10:48.740
make these things faster
without sacrificing quality.

01:10:49.240 --> 01:10:51.800
&gt;&gt; Might not be projecting there.

01:10:51.801 --> 01:10:54.960
&gt;&gt; It was projecting
a second ago.

01:11:03.220 --> 01:11:06.020
&gt;&gt; What number are you on there?

01:11:06.021 --> 01:11:15.450
You're on number 11 looks
like. There you go.

01:11:16.420 --> 01:11:23.820
&gt;&gt; All right. So, I'm going
to take as given that

01:11:23.821 --> 01:11:25.160
the right answer that we know

01:11:25.161 --> 01:11:27.250
a right answer for
inference and that

01:11:27.251 --> 01:11:28.570
it's basically Bayes rule is

01:11:28.571 --> 01:11:31.430
implemented in graphical models.

01:11:31.431 --> 01:11:34.585
But this answer is
incredibly expensive,

01:11:34.586 --> 01:11:35.840
and there are a lot
of people who have

01:11:35.841 --> 01:11:37.220
done a lot of great research on

01:11:37.221 --> 01:11:40.540
making it less
painfully expensive,

01:11:40.541 --> 01:11:46.050
but it's still pretty hard
to pay the cost sometimes.

01:11:46.051 --> 01:11:49.000
But if we can, it's
got lots of benefits,

01:11:49.001 --> 01:11:50.740
among them being that

01:11:50.741 --> 01:11:53.324
every node and edge in
your model has a semantics,

01:11:53.325 --> 01:11:55.790
probabilistic semantics,
which helps you

01:11:55.791 --> 01:11:56.970
design a model that

01:11:56.971 --> 01:11:58.700
takes the best advantage
of your data,

01:11:58.701 --> 01:12:00.510
helps you figure out what
your model is doing,

01:12:00.511 --> 01:12:03.005
debug it when
something goes wrong.

01:12:03.006 --> 01:12:05.200
On the other hand, unless

01:12:05.201 --> 01:12:07.035
you've been living under a rock,

01:12:07.036 --> 01:12:13.815
there are deep nets and
they are much faster,

01:12:13.816 --> 01:12:18.260
at least naively,
because they work,

01:12:18.261 --> 01:12:19.800
basically their
inference process is

01:12:19.801 --> 01:12:22.100
a whole bunch of
simple matrix operations,

01:12:22.101 --> 01:12:26.550
fixed quickly computed
non-linear functions.

01:12:26.551 --> 01:12:29.855
The training as well
can be very fast,

01:12:29.856 --> 01:12:32.310
it's basically
stochastic gradient descent,

01:12:32.311 --> 01:12:35.330
as opposed to Markov chain
Monte Carlo algorithms

01:12:35.331 --> 01:12:38.390
that are harder to

01:12:38.391 --> 01:12:42.190
design and may take
a while to converge.

01:12:42.191 --> 01:12:45.200
So, not much semantics
necessarily in these things,

01:12:45.201 --> 01:12:47.845
but they're fast and
they're very successful.

01:12:47.846 --> 01:12:49.670
So, the question I'm going
to ask in this talk,

01:12:49.671 --> 01:12:52.610
is can we get the
best of both worlds?

01:12:52.611 --> 01:12:55.000
So, can we design
deep nets that look more

01:12:55.001 --> 01:12:57.860
like graphical models,
or vice versa,

01:12:57.861 --> 01:13:02.120
and take advantage of
both the semantics

01:13:02.121 --> 01:13:06.940
and the scaling to big data.

01:13:06.941 --> 01:13:10.600
So, what we'd like is
a model format that's both

01:13:10.601 --> 01:13:14.045
practical in the sense of
a deep net, practical,

01:13:14.046 --> 01:13:15.920
you can train it
quickly on lots of

01:13:15.921 --> 01:13:18.860
data and get high performance,

01:13:18.861 --> 01:13:22.830
but we'd also like
to import some of

01:13:22.831 --> 01:13:28.690
the interpretation
from Bayes' rule.

01:13:29.710 --> 01:13:33.240
So, I'm going to take

01:13:33.241 --> 01:13:36.340
as an example inference
on a chain graph,

01:13:36.341 --> 01:13:37.800
just a whole bunch of

01:13:37.801 --> 01:13:40.390
random variables
connected in a line.

01:13:40.391 --> 01:13:42.770
So, for this chain graph,

01:13:42.771 --> 01:13:46.215
if the standard Bayes' net
is a hidden Markov model,

01:13:46.216 --> 01:13:48.675
but if you're
a neural network person,

01:13:48.676 --> 01:13:50.070
forget about
hidden Markov models,

01:13:50.071 --> 01:13:51.640
you're going to use a
recurrent neural network,

01:13:51.641 --> 01:13:55.225
maybe an LSTM, or a GRU,
or something like that.

01:13:55.226 --> 01:13:58.060
I want to say that RNNs are

01:13:58.061 --> 01:14:01.010
basically Bayes' nets already.

01:14:03.240 --> 01:14:05.570
But what do I mean that they're

01:14:05.571 --> 01:14:07.180
sort of Bayes nets already?

01:14:07.181 --> 01:14:13.210
So, if you're a recurrent
neural network processing

01:14:13.211 --> 01:14:15.515
along a sequence over time,

01:14:15.516 --> 01:14:17.680
at any given point,

01:14:17.681 --> 01:14:19.070
you have to have a notion of

01:14:19.071 --> 01:14:20.535
what the future is
going to look like.

01:14:20.536 --> 01:14:21.680
You have to be representing

01:14:21.681 --> 01:14:23.765
a probability distribution
over the future.

01:14:23.766 --> 01:14:26.210
That's true no matter what
your architecture is because

01:14:26.211 --> 01:14:28.745
as long as you're able to
predict the next observation,

01:14:28.746 --> 01:14:31.190
you can then sample
from that distribution,

01:14:31.191 --> 01:14:33.245
feed in your own prediction,

01:14:33.246 --> 01:14:35.310
and move yourself
forward a step and

01:14:35.311 --> 01:14:37.935
keep doing that until you
have a rollout of the future.

01:14:37.936 --> 01:14:40.355
So, you're representing
implicitly

01:14:40.356 --> 01:14:43.080
a prediction about
the future which

01:14:43.081 --> 01:14:48.110
is the point of Bayes' rule,

01:14:48.111 --> 01:14:51.600
is to give you a representation
of that distribution.

01:14:52.620 --> 01:14:55.785
The update rule for
recurrent neural network,

01:14:55.786 --> 01:14:57.350
you didn't learn
it by Bayes' rule,

01:14:57.351 --> 01:14:59.820
you learned it by let's
say backprop through time,

01:14:59.821 --> 01:15:05.210
but if you learned it so
that it performs well,

01:15:05.211 --> 01:15:08.370
it has to be doing
Bayes' rule approximately.

01:15:08.780 --> 01:15:12.320
Bayes' rule is the right answer

01:15:12.321 --> 01:15:14.175
so it has to be doing
approximately that.

01:15:14.176 --> 01:15:17.470
So, what we'd like

01:15:17.471 --> 01:15:19.995
to do is make this implicit
representation more

01:15:19.996 --> 01:15:22.730
explicit in order to maybe

01:15:22.731 --> 01:15:27.610
gain better performance and
behavior of these models.

01:15:27.611 --> 01:15:31.670
So, the first step

01:15:31.671 --> 01:15:32.880
is that I'm going
to suggest making

01:15:32.881 --> 01:15:35.910
this implicit representation
of the future more explicit.

01:15:35.911 --> 01:15:38.780
So, in addition to predicting

01:15:38.781 --> 01:15:40.190
your immediate next observation

01:15:40.191 --> 01:15:41.760
the way an RNN would always do,

01:15:41.761 --> 01:15:43.470
I'm going to suggest
that you should

01:15:43.471 --> 01:15:45.865
also predict statistics
of the future.

01:15:45.866 --> 01:15:51.025
So, things like moments of
the next few observations,

01:15:51.026 --> 01:15:53.075
or the count of

01:15:53.076 --> 01:15:54.695
the number of observations

01:15:54.696 --> 01:15:56.480
out of the next ten
that are all one,

01:15:56.481 --> 01:15:58.215
or things like that.

01:15:58.216 --> 01:16:00.935
If we have enough features,

01:16:00.936 --> 01:16:03.520
if you keep adding on

01:16:03.521 --> 01:16:06.810
independent predictions
that you have to make,

01:16:07.450 --> 01:16:09.950
after some point, you're going

01:16:09.951 --> 01:16:11.460
to get that your predictions

01:16:11.461 --> 01:16:15.095
are a 1:1 map from
your latent state.

01:16:15.096 --> 01:16:18.700
So, your latent state
vector of your RNN,

01:16:18.701 --> 01:16:20.140
uniquely determines

01:16:20.141 --> 01:16:22.015
your predictions and
your predictions,

01:16:22.016 --> 01:16:23.190
if there are enough of them,

01:16:23.191 --> 01:16:26.390
uniquely determine
the latent state vector.

01:16:26.730 --> 01:16:29.370
Therefore, they're
also a 1:1 map from

01:16:29.371 --> 01:16:30.640
this probability
distribution that

01:16:30.641 --> 01:16:31.915
we're trying to represent.

01:16:31.916 --> 01:16:36.105
So, this representation of

01:16:36.106 --> 01:16:38.070
a bunch of predictions of

01:16:38.071 --> 01:16:40.195
expected statistics
of the future,

01:16:40.196 --> 01:16:42.405
I'm going to call this
a predictive state.

01:16:42.406 --> 01:16:44.140
A state because
it's a 1:1 map from

01:16:44.141 --> 01:16:46.100
your actual latent state,

01:16:46.101 --> 01:16:48.675
and predictive because it's
a bunch of predictions.

01:16:48.676 --> 01:16:51.910
So, this idea sounds

01:16:51.911 --> 01:16:54.290
simple but it turns
out to be powerful.

01:16:54.291 --> 01:16:56.635
So, just a little intuition,

01:16:56.636 --> 01:16:59.490
one example I would like
to use is a video of

01:16:59.491 --> 01:17:02.175
a clock with its pendulum
swinging back and forth.

01:17:02.176 --> 01:17:04.140
If you wanted to make
a predictive state,

01:17:04.141 --> 01:17:05.440
you could directly predict

01:17:05.441 --> 01:17:07.505
the next two frames
of this video.

01:17:07.506 --> 01:17:09.570
Those two frames would

01:17:09.571 --> 01:17:11.860
encode the state of
the clock in the sense

01:17:11.861 --> 01:17:14.420
that they would give you

01:17:14.421 --> 01:17:15.630
enough to determine the position

01:17:15.631 --> 01:17:17.105
and velocity of the pendulum,

01:17:17.106 --> 01:17:20.295
which is the physical state
of the system.

01:17:20.296 --> 01:17:22.840
So, that's a visual example for

01:17:22.841 --> 01:17:25.670
people who like
examples and equations.

01:17:25.671 --> 01:17:29.300
Let's say we have
a hidden Markov model.

01:17:29.301 --> 01:17:31.030
So, we have a transition matrix T

01:17:31.031 --> 01:17:32.965
and an observation matrix O,

01:17:32.966 --> 01:17:35.140
and we look at the expected

01:17:35.141 --> 01:17:37.260
next observation given
the current states,

01:17:37.261 --> 01:17:40.345
so that's the observation
matrix times the latent state.

01:17:40.346 --> 01:17:42.480
The button next to observation,

01:17:42.481 --> 01:17:44.175
one time in one future,

01:17:44.176 --> 01:17:46.895
is O times T times your state.

01:17:46.896 --> 01:17:49.160
More steps in the future,

01:17:49.161 --> 01:17:51.010
you keep including
more copies of T,

01:17:51.011 --> 01:17:52.270
and if you stack enough of

01:17:52.271 --> 01:17:56.950
them so that that matrix
has full column rank,

01:17:56.951 --> 01:17:58.280
which you can always do

01:17:58.281 --> 01:18:02.135
if you don't have

01:18:02.136 --> 01:18:03.880
excess useless states in

01:18:03.881 --> 01:18:05.845
your in your hidden Markov model,

01:18:05.846 --> 01:18:07.500
if you have enough of that,

01:18:07.501 --> 01:18:10.110
then that vector of predictions
is a predictive state.

01:18:10.111 --> 01:18:11.860
So, just the expected observation

01:18:11.861 --> 01:18:14.465
over the next few time steps.

01:18:14.466 --> 01:18:16.940
So, you can add
this predictive state

01:18:16.941 --> 01:18:19.265
to a recurrent neural network,

01:18:19.266 --> 01:18:22.530
and doing so there's

01:18:22.531 --> 01:18:25.590
no necessary reason that it
has to make things better.

01:18:25.591 --> 01:18:26.690
It's an inductive bias,

01:18:26.691 --> 01:18:27.990
it's a statement
about the problems

01:18:27.991 --> 01:18:29.680
we expect to encounter.

01:18:29.681 --> 01:18:33.265
But empirically, it seems to
help prediction accuracy.

01:18:33.266 --> 01:18:36.440
So, the graph I have here is

01:18:36.441 --> 01:18:39.360
from this paper by
Venkatraman et al,

01:18:39.361 --> 01:18:41.140
it's Drew Bagnell's group,

01:18:41.141 --> 01:18:43.020
where if you use

01:18:43.021 --> 01:18:44.910
a particular algorithm for

01:18:44.911 --> 01:18:47.195
generating a
recurrent prediction,

01:18:47.196 --> 01:18:48.470
in this case of an action in

01:18:48.471 --> 01:18:49.700
a reinforcement learning problem,

01:18:49.701 --> 01:18:51.070
you get one level of quality.

01:18:51.071 --> 01:18:56.600
If you add as well
this predictive state notion,

01:18:56.601 --> 01:18:58.775
and train it to optimize that,

01:18:58.776 --> 01:19:01.140
this is an example of
multitask learning,

01:19:01.141 --> 01:19:07.070
and this inductive bias makes
your performance go up.

01:19:07.700 --> 01:19:10.560
Oh, but there's a problem, right?

01:19:10.561 --> 01:19:12.285
It's still a recurrent
neural network.

01:19:12.286 --> 01:19:14.110
When you train it, it's
still going to get

01:19:14.111 --> 01:19:16.245
you probably to a
local optimum, right?

01:19:16.246 --> 01:19:18.400
So I've heard a lot
of people claim

01:19:18.401 --> 01:19:20.730
that neural networks and
stochastic gradients

01:19:20.731 --> 01:19:22.950
have the nice property that

01:19:22.951 --> 01:19:25.845
all local optima are
approximately global,

01:19:25.846 --> 01:19:27.890
but the one exception won't,

01:19:27.891 --> 01:19:29.370
there may be more exceptions.

01:19:29.371 --> 01:19:32.295
But one certain exception to
that is recurrent networks.

01:19:32.296 --> 01:19:35.820
It is very, very possible
in my experience to get

01:19:35.821 --> 01:19:37.300
into a very bad local optima

01:19:37.301 --> 01:19:39.465
when you're training a
recurrent neural network.

01:19:39.466 --> 01:19:43.905
Okay. So, what are we going
to do to try and fix that?

01:19:43.906 --> 01:19:47.010
So, I'm going to propose that

01:19:47.011 --> 01:19:49.660
we should try and bootstrap
from supervised learning.

01:19:49.661 --> 01:19:51.590
So, empirically,

01:19:51.591 --> 01:19:53.390
all of the people making
these claims that

01:19:53.391 --> 01:19:55.400
all local optima or
global are talking about

01:19:55.401 --> 01:19:56.850
supervised learning problems or

01:19:56.851 --> 01:19:58.335
at least many of them are,

01:19:58.336 --> 01:20:00.020
and empirically it's true.

01:20:00.021 --> 01:20:02.560
There seemed to be fewer worries
about local optima when

01:20:02.561 --> 01:20:05.435
you're training
a supervised neural network.

01:20:05.436 --> 01:20:07.010
Theoretically, it's also true,

01:20:07.011 --> 01:20:09.670
for example, if you have
a linear predictor,

01:20:09.671 --> 01:20:11.960
a recurrent one can
have local optima but

01:20:11.961 --> 01:20:14.935
a supervised one
cannot. All right.

01:20:14.936 --> 01:20:17.035
So, let's try and
borrow that property.

01:20:17.036 --> 01:20:20.645
So, the bootstrap is
going to work like this.

01:20:20.646 --> 01:20:23.010
We're going to predict
our future features

01:20:23.011 --> 01:20:24.890
directly from a fixed window

01:20:24.891 --> 01:20:26.710
of history instead
of recurrently.

01:20:26.711 --> 01:20:27.580
So, that's going to be

01:20:27.581 --> 01:20:29.060
a supervised learning
problem, right?

01:20:29.061 --> 01:20:30.900
At any given time step
you have a past and

01:20:30.901 --> 01:20:33.385
the future and you predict
the future from the past,

01:20:33.386 --> 01:20:35.330
but it's suboptimal because you

01:20:35.331 --> 01:20:37.210
have a finite window
of memory, right?

01:20:37.211 --> 01:20:38.790
You can't remember anything for

01:20:38.791 --> 01:20:41.155
longer than the length
of that window.

01:20:41.156 --> 01:20:42.975
So, that's step one.

01:20:42.976 --> 01:20:44.550
Step two, now I've got

01:20:44.551 --> 01:20:46.580
a bunch of predictions
of the future and

01:20:46.581 --> 01:20:48.410
I'm going to take
my prediction that I

01:20:48.411 --> 01:20:50.655
made of the future at time t,

01:20:50.656 --> 01:20:52.780
and I'm going to add
that as an input

01:20:52.781 --> 01:20:55.020
when I predict the future
at time t plus 1, right?

01:20:55.021 --> 01:20:57.125
So I'm going to change
my predictions together,

01:20:57.126 --> 01:20:59.290
and this allows
infinite memory because

01:20:59.291 --> 01:21:02.584
a given observation will
influence my prediction,

01:21:02.585 --> 01:21:04.200
and that prediction
can then influence

01:21:04.201 --> 01:21:07.080
the next prediction in
the next one and the next one.

01:21:08.310 --> 01:21:11.650
So, it sounds like I'm making
things recurrent again,

01:21:11.651 --> 01:21:13.030
but I want to emphasize that

01:21:13.031 --> 01:21:15.610
I'm using past
predictions, right?

01:21:15.611 --> 01:21:16.960
I used an earlier version of

01:21:16.961 --> 01:21:18.525
my model to make
some predictions.

01:21:18.526 --> 01:21:22.000
I'm adding those to my training
set and their fixed,

01:21:22.001 --> 01:21:24.030
so I've not backpropagating
through them.

01:21:24.031 --> 01:21:25.610
So, I've gotten rid of

01:21:25.611 --> 01:21:27.795
recurrence but I've
introduced a new problem,

01:21:27.796 --> 01:21:29.430
which is that the
training distribution

01:21:29.431 --> 01:21:31.310
changes across iterations, right?

01:21:31.311 --> 01:21:33.230
If my model changes,

01:21:33.231 --> 01:21:34.580
then my predictions change,

01:21:34.581 --> 01:21:35.900
which changes now because

01:21:35.901 --> 01:21:38.720
my predictions are input
at the next time step.

01:21:38.721 --> 01:21:41.300
I've changed my training
distribution and so I've broken

01:21:41.301 --> 01:21:43.860
the assumptions of most
supervised learning algorithms.

01:21:43.861 --> 01:21:46.805
So, step three is going
to fix that problem.

01:21:46.806 --> 01:21:49.740
We're going to fix that problem
using imitation learning.

01:21:49.741 --> 01:21:54.835
So, a quick detour inference,

01:21:54.836 --> 01:21:56.930
you can think of it as a

01:21:56.931 --> 01:21:58.700
very special markup
decision process,

01:21:58.701 --> 01:22:00.780
a very special reinforcement
learning problem,

01:22:00.781 --> 01:22:03.945
where the state is the
predictions we've made so far.

01:22:03.946 --> 01:22:06.520
Our action is to make
another prediction,

01:22:06.521 --> 01:22:08.595
tack it onto the end of
our list of predictions,

01:22:08.596 --> 01:22:10.150
and our cost is the sum of

01:22:10.151 --> 01:22:12.040
the errors we make
in the predictions.

01:22:12.041 --> 01:22:15.280
So, learning to do
inference is the same

01:22:15.281 --> 01:22:16.740
as finding a good policy

01:22:16.741 --> 01:22:18.700
in this reinforcement
learning problem,

01:22:18.701 --> 01:22:20.840
but the nice thing
is that this is not

01:22:20.841 --> 01:22:22.965
a general reinforcement
learning problem.

01:22:22.966 --> 01:22:25.620
We actually have an expert if
we have training data that

01:22:25.621 --> 01:22:27.070
does a pretty good job that

01:22:27.071 --> 01:22:28.920
gives us a good policy
to start from.

01:22:28.921 --> 01:22:31.090
What that expert
does is every time

01:22:31.091 --> 01:22:33.855
the expert is called on
to make a new prediction,

01:22:33.856 --> 01:22:38.170
it copies that prediction
from our training set, right?

01:22:38.171 --> 01:22:40.905
So, that expert is a good policy,

01:22:40.906 --> 01:22:42.950
and so reinforcement learning,

01:22:42.951 --> 01:22:44.340
when you start with a good policy

01:22:44.341 --> 01:22:45.745
is actually much easier.

01:22:45.746 --> 01:22:49.155
It's probably impossible in
some sense if you don't,

01:22:49.156 --> 01:22:52.735
and probably possible
in a sense if you do.

01:22:52.736 --> 01:22:55.670
In particular, we can reduce
imitation learning to

01:22:55.671 --> 01:22:56.820
supervised learning which was

01:22:56.821 --> 01:22:58.490
the visitor outcome from before.

01:22:58.491 --> 01:22:59.710
We wanted to be able to

01:22:59.711 --> 01:23:01.895
bootstrap off of
supervised learning.

01:23:01.896 --> 01:23:03.850
So, it's going to do that

01:23:03.851 --> 01:23:07.630
by a framework called
approximate policy iteration,

01:23:07.631 --> 01:23:09.120
this captures a number of

01:23:09.121 --> 01:23:10.950
different imitation
learning algorithms.

01:23:10.951 --> 01:23:13.700
So, for those of you
who haven't seen it,

01:23:13.701 --> 01:23:16.360
exact policy iteration in

01:23:16.361 --> 01:23:18.479
your reinforcement
learning problem,

01:23:18.480 --> 01:23:20.400
you look at every single
state and action,

01:23:20.401 --> 01:23:22.995
so you should already see a
problem with this, right?

01:23:22.996 --> 01:23:25.280
Exact policy iteration
requires you to loop

01:23:25.281 --> 01:23:27.605
over all states and
actions in your problem.

01:23:27.606 --> 01:23:32.230
Calculate some statistics
of the current policy.

01:23:32.231 --> 01:23:34.850
How well it performs
starting from

01:23:34.851 --> 01:23:36.990
each state-action pair and

01:23:36.991 --> 01:23:38.920
some quantities you can

01:23:38.921 --> 01:23:41.035
compute from those
including the advantage,

01:23:41.036 --> 01:23:44.150
which is the difference
between the quality of

01:23:44.151 --> 01:23:46.400
a given action at
a given state and

01:23:46.401 --> 01:23:49.720
the quality of the best
action at that state.

01:23:50.620 --> 01:23:53.275
So, we choose then

01:23:53.276 --> 01:23:55.700
a policy which switches
to the best action.

01:23:55.701 --> 01:23:58.369
The policy which
optimizes the advantage,

01:23:58.370 --> 01:24:00.760
meaning the policy which picks

01:24:00.761 --> 01:24:02.520
the action which has

01:24:02.521 --> 01:24:04.370
the smallest difference
from the best action,

01:24:04.371 --> 01:24:06.440
namely, the best action
at every state.

01:24:06.441 --> 01:24:08.540
Now, this doesn't work
in a real sized problem

01:24:08.541 --> 01:24:12.595
because you can't iterate
over all state action pairs.

01:24:12.596 --> 01:24:16.430
You can't exactly calculate
the advantage function,

01:24:16.431 --> 01:24:18.230
you have to estimate it somehow,

01:24:18.231 --> 01:24:20.470
and you can't freely choose
a new policy, right?

01:24:20.471 --> 01:24:22.270
So I just said that

01:24:22.271 --> 01:24:26.050
my new policy separately set
its value at every state,

01:24:26.051 --> 01:24:27.440
but I can't do that,
I have to work in

01:24:27.441 --> 01:24:29.440
some hypothesis class
of policies.

01:24:29.441 --> 01:24:30.750
So, instead, we're going to use

01:24:30.751 --> 01:24:32.590
approximate policy iteration.

01:24:32.591 --> 01:24:37.950
So, to do that what
we're going to

01:24:37.951 --> 01:24:41.490
do is effectively
approximate each

01:24:41.491 --> 01:24:43.180
of the steps of
policy iteration instead of

01:24:43.181 --> 01:24:45.370
exactly computing this advantage,

01:24:45.371 --> 01:24:47.930
we're going to estimate
the advantage function.

01:24:47.931 --> 01:24:51.630
Instead of exactly
computing this new policy,

01:24:51.631 --> 01:24:55.120
we're going to update
our policy within

01:24:55.121 --> 01:24:58.580
some hypothesis class
to improve a criteria.

01:24:58.581 --> 01:25:01.650
The same criterion we were
trying to improve before,

01:25:01.651 --> 01:25:06.670
I just want to highlight,

01:25:06.671 --> 01:25:10.750
so what we want is
the expected value under the,

01:25:10.751 --> 01:25:12.735
if we follow the new policy

01:25:12.736 --> 01:25:14.820
and we look at the states
and actions we see when

01:25:14.821 --> 01:25:17.950
we're following that new policy
of the advantage that

01:25:17.951 --> 01:25:21.230
we see of that state action pair.

01:25:21.231 --> 01:25:23.520
So, we want to effectively

01:25:23.521 --> 01:25:25.630
reduce the number of
times we're taking

01:25:25.631 --> 01:25:27.665
suboptimal actions
in a given state

01:25:27.666 --> 01:25:30.815
and reduce the amount by
which they're suboptimal.

01:25:30.816 --> 01:25:33.999
So, to instantiate, this
is a meta-algorithm,

01:25:34.000 --> 01:25:37.750
you can't code it in
Python or Ray or any of

01:25:37.751 --> 01:25:42.550
these systems without saying

01:25:42.551 --> 01:25:43.725
how we're going to estimate

01:25:43.726 --> 01:25:46.995
the advantage and how we're
going to do the update.

01:25:46.996 --> 01:25:49.790
But supposing that you
could implement it,

01:25:49.791 --> 01:25:51.814
you would get
some nice guarantees.

01:25:51.815 --> 01:25:56.155
I'm not going to go through
the analysis but basically,

01:25:56.156 --> 01:25:58.550
what it says is
that, so this here,

01:25:58.551 --> 01:26:01.205
this expectation is what
you're trying to optimize.

01:26:01.206 --> 01:26:03.770
If you're able to
get it below zero,

01:26:03.771 --> 01:26:05.390
then you're guaranteed that

01:26:05.391 --> 01:26:07.390
your new policy is
better than the old one.

01:26:07.391 --> 01:26:09.320
That's something that you
can empirically measure,

01:26:09.321 --> 01:26:11.930
so you know when
you're succeeding.

01:26:11.931 --> 01:26:13.890
And sort of qualitatively,

01:26:13.891 --> 01:26:15.360
you'll succeed when you have

01:26:15.361 --> 01:26:17.370
a policy that's not the
best in your class, right?

01:26:17.371 --> 01:26:18.380
You can't expect to improve

01:26:18.381 --> 01:26:20.080
it if you already have the best.

01:26:20.081 --> 01:26:25.415
If the class is rich enough
that it can represent

01:26:25.416 --> 01:26:31.520
an improvement and
basically training succeeds,

01:26:31.521 --> 01:26:32.710
that you're actually able to

01:26:32.711 --> 01:26:34.880
find within
your hypothesis class,

01:26:34.881 --> 01:26:37.680
this policy that
causes an improvement.

01:26:39.750 --> 01:26:43.340
So, there are a couple
of specific algorithms

01:26:43.341 --> 01:26:46.140
within this class that
I wanted to point out.

01:26:46.141 --> 01:26:47.980
So, one of them is called DAgger.

01:26:47.981 --> 01:26:50.350
I just wanted to
point out this is

01:26:50.351 --> 01:26:53.320
not the same as the DAgger
that Mike presented.

01:26:53.321 --> 01:26:54.760
I also wanted to point out,

01:26:54.761 --> 01:26:56.680
I think we got the name first.

01:26:56.681 --> 01:27:04.060
So, anyway, but we capitalize
the first two letters,

01:27:04.061 --> 01:27:06.750
so you can tell the difference
between the algorithms.

01:27:06.751 --> 01:27:09.110
So, it stands for
Dataset Aggregation.

01:27:09.111 --> 01:27:14.735
So, the details aren't important,

01:27:14.736 --> 01:27:17.930
except that what you
wind up doing is solving

01:27:17.931 --> 01:27:19.970
a classification problem on

01:27:19.971 --> 01:27:23.475
every iteration using what's
called a no-regret learner.

01:27:23.476 --> 01:27:26.105
So, no-regret learners
are very nice.

01:27:26.106 --> 01:27:28.750
If you're interested in
machine learning theory,

01:27:28.751 --> 01:27:31.235
you should read up on
them, they're cool.

01:27:31.236 --> 01:27:33.990
But the key thing to know about

01:27:33.991 --> 01:27:36.795
them is that neural nets
are not one of them.

01:27:36.796 --> 01:27:39.980
So, what this lets you
do is if you're willing

01:27:39.981 --> 01:27:42.750
to restrict yourself to
the class of no-regret learners,

01:27:42.751 --> 01:27:46.090
this gives you a way that has

01:27:46.091 --> 01:27:48.130
some nice results you

01:27:48.131 --> 01:27:51.020
can prove about
the improvement in the policy.

01:27:51.950 --> 01:27:56.315
So, the other one I wanted
to point out is instead

01:27:56.316 --> 01:27:59.720
of completely solving
this classification problem

01:27:59.721 --> 01:28:01.020
using a no-regret learner,

01:28:01.021 --> 01:28:02.900
it's also possible to
write an algorithm that

01:28:02.901 --> 01:28:05.085
does a gradient step.

01:28:05.086 --> 01:28:09.000
Much like SGD but with
a slightly different rule for,

01:28:09.001 --> 01:28:11.175
you are calculating
the gradient of something else.

01:28:11.176 --> 01:28:16.940
So, another catchy acronym

01:28:16.941 --> 01:28:19.570
for this one's called AggreVated.

01:28:19.630 --> 01:28:21.865
Differentiable,

01:28:21.866 --> 01:28:25.345
that's the D version of
aggregate values to imitate.

01:28:25.346 --> 01:28:28.710
One of my collaborators has
an excessive fondness of

01:28:28.711 --> 01:28:30.240
good acronyms as you can

01:28:30.241 --> 01:28:32.275
probably tell from
the past two slides.

01:28:32.276 --> 01:28:34.480
Anyway, so the point here is

01:28:34.481 --> 01:28:36.665
that you don't have
to restrict yourself

01:28:36.666 --> 01:28:38.400
with this newer version
of the algorithm

01:28:38.401 --> 01:28:41.380
to a no-regret learner,

01:28:41.381 --> 01:28:43.755
you can do it with
any differentiable learner,

01:28:43.756 --> 01:28:45.870
any learner where you

01:28:45.871 --> 01:28:47.550
can make your gradient
descent update step.

01:28:47.551 --> 01:28:52.210
So, in both of these cases,

01:28:52.211 --> 01:28:54.840
what we've done is we've taken
a supervised learner and

01:28:54.841 --> 01:29:02.145
we've trained our neural network

01:29:02.146 --> 01:29:04.060
not by backpropagation
through time,

01:29:04.061 --> 01:29:05.645
not as a recurrent
neural network,

01:29:05.646 --> 01:29:08.885
but by reduction to
a supervised learning algorithm.

01:29:08.886 --> 01:29:11.560
Empirically, this
actually seems to help.

01:29:11.561 --> 01:29:15.840
So, the print is
probably too small to

01:29:15.841 --> 01:29:19.800
see, but for example,

01:29:19.801 --> 01:29:24.370
on this plot here,

01:29:29.160 --> 01:29:32.469
so if you start from
random initialization,

01:29:32.470 --> 01:29:34.550
you train several
different algorithms and

01:29:34.551 --> 01:29:37.335
they all get up to
some level of performance.

01:29:37.336 --> 01:29:39.970
Then, for example,
this green line is

01:29:39.971 --> 01:29:42.380
the vanilla version of
the algorithm that I just

01:29:42.381 --> 01:29:49.520
described and you start

01:29:49.521 --> 01:29:52.840
with a smart initialization
and you can do

01:29:52.841 --> 01:29:54.440
more gradient descent
to get yourself

01:29:54.441 --> 01:29:57.620
better and it winds up in
a better local optimum.

01:29:57.621 --> 01:30:02.815
All right. So, how much time
do I have left? I'm done.

01:30:02.816 --> 01:30:05.100
Okay. So, in that case,

01:30:05.101 --> 01:30:07.555
I think I'll stop here

01:30:07.556 --> 01:30:10.600
and just summarize and
say that basically what

01:30:10.601 --> 01:30:14.035
I'm trying to do is

01:30:14.036 --> 01:30:15.790
to figure out how to

01:30:15.791 --> 01:30:18.180
take these deep networks
that are so efficient,

01:30:18.181 --> 01:30:19.455
so useful in practice,

01:30:19.456 --> 01:30:23.480
and make them both on
semantics that's more like

01:30:23.481 --> 01:30:26.260
a graphical model
and to try and gain

01:30:26.261 --> 01:30:27.670
some benefit from doing that not

01:30:27.671 --> 01:30:29.380
just the benefit of
interpretability,

01:30:29.381 --> 01:30:32.310
but benefits in actual
practical performance as well.

01:30:32.311 --> 01:30:34.880
So, thank you.

01:30:34.881 --> 01:30:37.000
&gt;&gt; We are going to

01:30:37.001 --> 01:30:39.465
request other speakers
to actually come up,

01:30:39.466 --> 01:30:41.010
and then if anyone in

01:30:41.011 --> 01:30:42.640
the audience has questions,
please raise your hands.

01:30:42.641 --> 01:30:45.650
We have mic owners here
who will get a mic to you.

01:30:47.790 --> 01:30:50.085
&gt;&gt; All right. Where do
you want us to stand?

01:30:50.086 --> 01:30:52.660
&gt;&gt; Oh, actually maybe
there in the spotlight.

01:30:52.661 --> 01:30:54.730
&gt;&gt; In the spotlight, of course.

01:30:54.731 --> 01:31:00.010
&gt;&gt; As we're figuring this out,

01:31:00.011 --> 01:31:01.340
they're still questions from

01:31:01.341 --> 01:31:02.780
the audience maybe
I can start off.

01:31:02.781 --> 01:31:05.395
I had a question about
strong guarantees.

01:31:05.396 --> 01:31:08.875
So, it looks like
there is this trend.

01:31:08.876 --> 01:31:11.020
Finally, we've reached
a point where they're

01:31:11.021 --> 01:31:15.340
programming the tools including

01:31:15.341 --> 01:31:18.590
programming language support
that will allow

01:31:18.591 --> 01:31:22.500
us to hold programs responsible
for what they're doing.

01:31:22.501 --> 01:31:24.995
Strong guarantees from programs.

01:31:24.996 --> 01:31:26.980
There's always been
this move, model-checking,

01:31:26.981 --> 01:31:28.450
and systematic testing, but

01:31:28.451 --> 01:31:30.630
no verification in
terms of approve.

01:31:30.631 --> 01:31:32.310
So, you fail at

01:31:32.311 --> 01:31:35.520
the compile step as opposed
to running your program.

01:31:35.521 --> 01:31:39.840
Now, unless you're hit
with this wave of models,

01:31:39.841 --> 01:31:42.260
that can potentially
replace programs,

01:31:42.261 --> 01:31:44.220
are learning from samples.

01:31:44.221 --> 01:31:47.710
It's not clear how
robust they are.

01:31:47.711 --> 01:31:51.485
So, is this taking
us back a step?

01:31:51.486 --> 01:31:53.790
We've just begun to
take a step forward,

01:31:53.791 --> 01:31:56.135
and you want to be
taken a step back.

01:31:56.136 --> 01:31:59.510
Do you have an opinion on this?

01:32:00.900 --> 01:32:03.910
&gt;&gt; Let me push back
against software 2.0.

01:32:03.911 --> 01:32:05.500
I mean I think it's not

01:32:05.501 --> 01:32:07.955
a replacement of
software 1.0 at all.

01:32:07.956 --> 01:32:11.080
It's just a different tool.
So, it's a commodity.

01:32:11.081 --> 01:32:12.730
You can get a neural net
to learn how

01:32:12.731 --> 01:32:15.600
to maybe recognize fro
classid image to visual scenes,

01:32:15.601 --> 01:32:17.360
and say there's a person
in them or not.

01:32:17.361 --> 01:32:19.245
You can do that
with software 2.0.

01:32:19.246 --> 01:32:21.120
Otherwise, gather all the data

01:32:21.121 --> 01:32:23.130
of all possible scenes
and all possible people,

01:32:23.131 --> 01:32:23.950
and put them into
a big neural net and

01:32:23.951 --> 01:32:24.990
they get better and

01:32:24.991 --> 01:32:27.260
better over the years
and shape that.

01:32:27.261 --> 01:32:31.030
Okay, but that thing has
done no abstraction.

01:32:31.031 --> 01:32:35.370
It's not a predictable module
for all purposes.

01:32:35.371 --> 01:32:37.855
It's just a little piece. It
sounds like an accelerator.

01:32:37.856 --> 01:32:40.495
So, I don't think of it
as replacing at all.

01:32:40.496 --> 01:32:45.230
That said if you have
static versus dynamic,

01:32:45.231 --> 01:32:47.030
I still think dynamic
is that way to go.

01:32:47.031 --> 01:32:48.500
You're not going to
see all the things

01:32:48.501 --> 01:32:49.630
that can happen in
the real world.

01:32:49.631 --> 01:32:53.915
We interface with the real world
a static program.

01:32:53.916 --> 01:32:56.020
You can prove that
the things could do

01:32:56.021 --> 01:32:57.660
exactly the thing
you hoped it'll do,

01:32:57.661 --> 01:32:59.910
but what you're hoping
to do is exactly wrong.

01:32:59.911 --> 01:33:01.390
So, you get a great proof
that it's going

01:33:01.391 --> 01:33:03.245
to go to the wrong thing.

01:33:03.246 --> 01:33:05.920
So, no. Computer scientists

01:33:05.921 --> 01:33:07.390
have got to realize that
the next wave they're

01:33:07.391 --> 01:33:08.600
interfacing with
the real world with all

01:33:08.601 --> 01:33:10.240
of its vagaries and

01:33:10.241 --> 01:33:11.530
not proving that what happens

01:33:11.531 --> 01:33:13.535
inside the computer is what
you thought would happen.

01:33:13.536 --> 01:33:15.640
&gt;&gt; I think there's
some really exciting work

01:33:15.641 --> 01:33:17.320
that's going on at
Stanford from Dawn Song,

01:33:17.321 --> 01:33:18.770
who just came over, and

01:33:18.771 --> 01:33:19.720
other folks who are working

01:33:19.721 --> 01:33:21.780
on validation and verification.

01:33:21.781 --> 01:33:23.690
They call themselves
the AI safety group.

01:33:23.691 --> 01:33:25.360
They're trying to
say even if you have

01:33:25.361 --> 01:33:26.410
a black box view of what's

01:33:26.411 --> 01:33:27.775
going on underneath the covers,

01:33:27.776 --> 01:33:31.455
can you do higher level sort
of proofs on it?

01:33:31.456 --> 01:33:33.050
So, Michael [inaudible] ,

01:33:33.051 --> 01:33:34.310
who actually worries about planes

01:33:34.311 --> 01:33:35.960
turning left right and center,

01:33:35.961 --> 01:33:38.200
actually has some
pretty exciting stuff

01:33:38.201 --> 01:33:39.840
where he's able to say, "Yes.

01:33:39.841 --> 01:33:41.650
Indeed. We can verify

01:33:41.651 --> 01:33:43.450
that the plane when it claims
it's going to turn left,

01:33:43.451 --> 01:33:44.715
for control theory will turn

01:33:44.716 --> 01:33:46.410
right." So, that's one issue.

01:33:46.411 --> 01:33:47.700
So, there are
a whole host of places

01:33:47.701 --> 01:33:49.080
where people have
paid the cost for

01:33:49.081 --> 01:33:50.990
verification to build
software products

01:33:50.991 --> 01:33:52.545
for a long period of time,

01:33:52.546 --> 01:33:55.560
but the trend seems to be going
the other way in my mind.

01:33:55.561 --> 01:33:56.810
I mean when Python

01:33:56.811 --> 01:33:58.330
first came around
remember everyone said,

01:33:58.331 --> 01:33:59.560
"Oh. It doesn't have types.

01:33:59.561 --> 01:34:01.110
It doesn't cache anything
at compile-time.

01:34:01.111 --> 01:34:02.045
No one's going to use it."

01:34:02.046 --> 01:34:03.830
But the amount of stuff
that's built on top of

01:34:03.831 --> 01:34:05.620
it is truly staggering.

01:34:05.621 --> 01:34:08.985
Right? So as Mike
says, and I agree,

01:34:08.986 --> 01:34:10.530
the pieces that people are

01:34:10.531 --> 01:34:12.315
building right now with
these neural nets,

01:34:12.316 --> 01:34:14.815
they are kind of
these individual components.

01:34:14.816 --> 01:34:16.230
But those individual
components and

01:34:16.231 --> 01:34:17.660
those individual
functional teams are

01:34:17.661 --> 01:34:19.150
building most of the applications

01:34:19.151 --> 01:34:21.255
that are being built
at the hyperscalers.

01:34:21.256 --> 01:34:23.690
So, I think in one way where
we're in agreement is we

01:34:23.691 --> 01:34:26.010
need abstractions to be able
to compose those things,

01:34:26.011 --> 01:34:27.885
and understand them, and
validate what's going on.

01:34:27.886 --> 01:34:29.920
We need some notion
of modularity.

01:34:29.921 --> 01:34:31.685
But I think it's
a new notion of modularity.

01:34:31.686 --> 01:34:35.520
It's not the traditional
software 1.0 wasn't that great.

01:34:35.521 --> 01:34:37.260
It's not like we solved all
these problems and didn't

01:34:37.261 --> 01:34:39.315
have bugs and understood
what was going on.

01:34:39.316 --> 01:34:40.645
It was kind of a shit show,

01:34:40.646 --> 01:34:41.810
I think is the technical term.

01:34:41.811 --> 01:34:45.390
So, the comparison in
the bar is pretty low.

01:34:45.391 --> 01:34:47.270
I think the interesting
question and

01:34:47.271 --> 01:34:49.250
where we probably are
in agreement is: Okay.

01:34:49.251 --> 01:34:50.930
We have these great
pattern-matching engines.

01:34:50.931 --> 01:34:54.260
They allow us to do
certain tasks that are just too

01:34:54.261 --> 01:34:58.370
difficult to program
tediously line by line.

01:34:58.371 --> 01:35:00.730
How do we build them
into realistic systems

01:35:00.731 --> 01:35:02.285
that don't fall over in the wild?

01:35:02.286 --> 01:35:04.340
Right now, we have Machine
Learning systems that are

01:35:04.341 --> 01:35:06.670
deployed that make
stupid errors day-in

01:35:06.671 --> 01:35:08.780
day-out hundreds of
times a day where you

01:35:08.781 --> 01:35:11.245
can't imagine why
they get that wrong.

01:35:11.246 --> 01:35:12.815
So, we got to do something there,

01:35:12.816 --> 01:35:14.000
and I think Mike's right.

01:35:14.001 --> 01:35:15.260
It's probably going to be

01:35:15.261 --> 01:35:16.600
some amount of abstraction

01:35:16.601 --> 01:35:17.855
of how do we program
these things.

01:35:17.856 --> 01:35:18.950
But you're no longer programming

01:35:18.951 --> 01:35:21.505
like jumps and for-loops
and things like that,

01:35:21.506 --> 01:35:23.250
you're programming at a much
higher level of abstraction,

01:35:23.251 --> 01:35:25.905
and it's a challenge for
computer science to get there.

01:35:25.906 --> 01:35:30.015
&gt;&gt; My phone fails to auto
complete my name every time.

01:35:30.016 --> 01:35:32.780
So, yeah, that's an example.

01:35:32.781 --> 01:35:33.740
&gt;&gt; That's why I
don't want to spend

01:35:33.741 --> 01:35:34.760
on Twitter a lot recently.

01:35:34.761 --> 01:35:35.260
&gt;&gt; Okay.

01:35:35.261 --> 01:35:37.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah. It's pretty simple.

01:35:37.211 --> 01:35:37.555
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:35:37.556 --> 01:35:37.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:35:37.931 --> 01:35:41.230
&gt;&gt; So, I have to
agree with this idea

01:35:41.231 --> 01:35:42.390
that abstraction is going to

01:35:42.391 --> 01:35:44.700
be the really important thing.

01:35:44.701 --> 01:35:45.920
I don't think we're there yet,

01:35:45.921 --> 01:35:47.265
I don't think we know

01:35:47.266 --> 01:35:48.865
the right way that

01:35:48.866 --> 01:35:50.860
AI systems are going
to do abstraction,

01:35:50.861 --> 01:35:52.200
but you're going to
be able to compose

01:35:52.201 --> 01:35:54.000
AI systems with one another.

01:35:54.001 --> 01:35:58.120
I think this is, it's
a grand frontier for research

01:35:58.121 --> 01:35:58.930
because I think there are a bunch

01:35:58.931 --> 01:36:00.130
of really interesting ideas

01:36:00.131 --> 01:36:03.000
out there but nobody's solved it.

01:36:03.570 --> 01:36:06.590
&gt;&gt; Are there questions
in the audience?

01:36:06.670 --> 01:36:09.520
Someone's waving at me
telling me we're out of time,

01:36:09.521 --> 01:36:11.440
but I have one more question.

01:36:11.441 --> 01:36:15.640
So, this is about
sort of benchmarks.

01:36:15.641 --> 01:36:16.990
So, it looks like
there are a lot of

01:36:16.991 --> 01:36:20.495
new algorithms people
are coming up with

01:36:20.496 --> 01:36:23.060
highlighting their
awesomeness including

01:36:23.061 --> 01:36:26.470
new hardware that people
are coming up with.

01:36:26.471 --> 01:36:29.070
People have begun to take
the baby steps of defining

01:36:29.071 --> 01:36:31.820
benchmarks so that it can
be some common substrate

01:36:31.821 --> 01:36:38.140
that you can use for comparison.

01:36:38.141 --> 01:36:40.555
So, you might have heard of

01:36:40.556 --> 01:36:43.500
MLPERF as being an example
where we're collating

01:36:43.501 --> 01:36:45.020
a bunch of working models

01:36:45.021 --> 01:36:46.570
that different people
can actually

01:36:46.571 --> 01:36:51.190
use as a benchmark.

01:36:51.191 --> 01:36:54.580
But that's just a
first small step,

01:36:54.581 --> 01:36:56.050
do you have thoughts in what

01:36:56.051 --> 01:36:57.785
the benchmark should look like?

01:36:57.786 --> 01:37:00.110
What's missing in there that

01:37:00.111 --> 01:37:02.430
ought to be there from like
an individual perspective,

01:37:02.431 --> 01:37:03.980
and not just individual

01:37:03.981 --> 01:37:05.290
algorithms like
image classification?

01:37:05.291 --> 01:37:07.630
&gt;&gt; One thing I'll plug
there is Matei Zaharia,

01:37:07.631 --> 01:37:10.250
and Peter Bailis did a huge
amount of work on DAWNBench,

01:37:10.251 --> 01:37:12.010
which came out and
now is moving in to

01:37:12.011 --> 01:37:13.925
be a part of the MLPERF effort.

01:37:13.926 --> 01:37:15.860
I think they had a

01:37:15.861 --> 01:37:17.795
great visionary there
that I understand about.

01:37:17.796 --> 01:37:19.605
Trying to hold people's feet
to the fire and

01:37:19.606 --> 01:37:22.160
understand how to
bring back that level

01:37:22.161 --> 01:37:24.500
of indirection or
abstraction that allows

01:37:24.501 --> 01:37:25.720
people to optimize models

01:37:25.721 --> 01:37:27.240
independently of
their applications.

01:37:27.241 --> 01:37:28.750
We might need
a great reference to this,

01:37:28.751 --> 01:37:30.490
and I agree the current set of

01:37:30.491 --> 01:37:32.230
end-to-end models is
kind of a disaster

01:37:32.231 --> 01:37:33.790
because you can't work on

01:37:33.791 --> 01:37:36.185
one component without affecting
every other component,

01:37:36.186 --> 01:37:37.550
and so benchmarks are one way

01:37:37.551 --> 01:37:38.890
to potentially drive like "Okay.

01:37:38.891 --> 01:37:40.735
We're going to
understand this level."

01:37:40.736 --> 01:37:42.950
I haven't given it as much
thought as those folks.

01:37:42.951 --> 01:37:44.220
You should definitely
ask Matei. I think he's

01:37:44.221 --> 01:37:45.750
giving a talk in
the next session.

01:37:45.751 --> 01:37:47.385
He's thought about this
a lot more deeply.

01:37:47.386 --> 01:37:49.260
But the current
benchmarks are kind of

01:37:49.261 --> 01:37:51.375
just the traditional horse races.

01:37:51.376 --> 01:37:54.150
They're just "Hey, this
looks like a spec benchmark.

01:37:54.151 --> 01:37:55.635
We want to see how fast we go."

01:37:55.636 --> 01:37:57.090
There's nothing about
sample efficiency.

01:37:57.091 --> 01:37:58.835
There's nothing about
data augmentation.

01:37:58.836 --> 01:38:01.110
There's just the inklings of
what's going to be around

01:38:01.111 --> 01:38:03.110
mobile performance which is
a huge and massive area.

01:38:03.111 --> 01:38:04.600
That's something
that's coming online.

01:38:04.601 --> 01:38:06.960
So, benchmarking
that's closer to what

01:38:06.961 --> 01:38:10.405
people are building is
an obvious direction to go.

01:38:10.406 --> 01:38:13.825
I hope that [inaudible]
is one of many efforts.

01:38:13.826 --> 01:38:15.990
I hope it doesn't get controlled
by just corporations,

01:38:15.991 --> 01:38:17.260
and assume I've had a great tweet

01:38:17.261 --> 01:38:18.560
about exactly that point.

01:38:18.561 --> 01:38:20.760
That academics really
should be playing

01:38:20.761 --> 01:38:23.240
a bigger role at being
neutral arbiters of "Hey,

01:38:23.241 --> 01:38:24.400
here's what's interesting
and exciting."

01:38:24.401 --> 01:38:25.760
Matei and Peter deserve

01:38:25.761 --> 01:38:27.420
a lot of credit for going
out there and doing

01:38:27.421 --> 01:38:30.540
a lot of unglamorous work
to get that thing running.

01:38:30.541 --> 01:38:31.920
&gt;&gt; So, on that note, I
think like benchmarks are

01:38:31.921 --> 01:38:33.500
important in terms of
defining the workload,

01:38:33.501 --> 01:38:35.300
but also in terms of
defining what are

01:38:35.301 --> 01:38:37.470
the metrics that people
should actually be measuring.

01:38:37.471 --> 01:38:39.170
I think often there's a lot

01:38:39.171 --> 01:38:41.055
of games that can be
played in terms of,

01:38:41.056 --> 01:38:42.560
let's say, you're
running the same net.

01:38:42.561 --> 01:38:44.370
But whether or not you
batch or not batch,

01:38:44.371 --> 01:38:46.310
whether or not you're
deciding how much do

01:38:46.311 --> 01:38:48.595
you rely on off-chip memory,
on-chip memory,

01:38:48.596 --> 01:38:50.265
thinking more from
the mobile side of things,

01:38:50.266 --> 01:38:52.560
it's really important to
give a comprehensive set of

01:38:52.561 --> 01:38:55.420
metrics to validate
those particular benchmarks.

01:38:55.421 --> 01:38:56.290
Not just the workload,

01:38:56.291 --> 01:38:57.530
but what are the
metrics that should

01:38:57.531 --> 01:39:01.100
be evaluated as well should
also be incorporated.

01:39:01.350 --> 01:39:03.540
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I, first of all,

01:39:03.541 --> 01:39:04.730
just need to emphasize again.

01:39:04.731 --> 01:39:06.550
This is not one little field.

01:39:06.551 --> 01:39:08.085
This is not databases.

01:39:08.086 --> 01:39:11.210
This is huge it's all about

01:39:11.211 --> 01:39:12.950
everything having
to do with all of

01:39:12.951 --> 01:39:14.985
AB testing is included in here,

01:39:14.986 --> 01:39:17.490
all of control theory
is included,

01:39:17.491 --> 01:39:20.260
all of natural
language processing.

01:39:20.261 --> 01:39:22.155
It's just this is massive.

01:39:22.156 --> 01:39:24.190
It can't be a few benchmarks.

01:39:24.191 --> 01:39:27.495
So, with Chris, I
totally agree on this.

01:39:27.496 --> 01:39:28.500
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:39:28.501 --> 01:39:30.330
&gt;&gt; It's good if there's a need
for a lot of them and lots

01:39:30.331 --> 01:39:32.560
of further testing of
various aspects of it.

01:39:32.561 --> 01:39:35.260
I agree with her too. It is

01:39:35.261 --> 01:39:36.870
about not just what's
inside the computer.

01:39:36.871 --> 01:39:38.190
It's about what's
in the real world.

01:39:38.191 --> 01:39:39.390
The loss function should be

01:39:39.391 --> 01:39:40.440
things that people care about,

01:39:40.441 --> 01:39:41.840
so they should have
utilities there.

01:39:41.841 --> 01:39:43.830
So that they should be worried
about dangers that you can

01:39:43.831 --> 01:39:45.980
get into the situation
you're going.

01:39:45.981 --> 01:39:47.170
I mentioned provenance early,

01:39:47.171 --> 01:39:50.195
and we just kind of bring
that back out a little bit.

01:39:50.196 --> 01:39:52.220
You know, database people are

01:39:52.221 --> 01:39:54.180
the only ones I know about
who I talk about this.

01:39:54.181 --> 01:39:55.880
But I think they have so
much more they could do with

01:39:55.881 --> 01:39:58.795
it because provenance is that

01:39:58.796 --> 01:40:01.010
a piece of data should
be tagged with where it

01:40:01.011 --> 01:40:07.385
was found and various
metadata about it.

01:40:07.386 --> 01:40:08.815
In a statistical setting,

01:40:08.816 --> 01:40:11.320
it's question is how relevant
is it? So, you're a doctor.

01:40:11.321 --> 01:40:13.165
You're trying to decide
treatment for me.

01:40:13.166 --> 01:40:15.000
Well, you look around, is
there any other people I

01:40:15.001 --> 01:40:16.650
have ever seen like
this person? All right.

01:40:16.651 --> 01:40:18.140
That's provenance, and how

01:40:18.141 --> 01:40:20.030
relevant is that data I
collect on that person,

01:40:20.031 --> 01:40:22.170
and that person's living
over there in Africa,

01:40:22.171 --> 01:40:23.270
is it relevant or is it there?

01:40:23.271 --> 01:40:26.300
That whole collection
which is relevant for a

01:40:26.301 --> 01:40:27.870
decision in a particular context

01:40:27.871 --> 01:40:30.035
that's provenance broadly writ.

01:40:30.036 --> 01:40:33.790
So, thinking about benchmarks
for that I [inaudible].

01:40:33.791 --> 01:40:36.540
But it is a key part. It's one

01:40:36.541 --> 01:40:38.535
of a 100 key parts
of the problem.

01:40:38.536 --> 01:40:40.590
So, yes, we got to be
quantitative and all that,

01:40:40.591 --> 01:40:41.810
but let's definitely not get to

01:40:41.811 --> 01:40:44.060
a few benchmarks and
have them be neural nets

01:40:44.061 --> 01:40:45.970
applied to images
too soon because

01:40:45.971 --> 01:40:48.230
that would really kill
off the bigger picture.

01:40:48.231 --> 01:40:49.710
&gt;&gt; One thing I should
also plug in there

01:40:49.711 --> 01:40:50.750
which is an honest
thing is there's

01:40:50.751 --> 01:40:52.960
a great paper by Percy
Liang and his co-authors.

01:40:52.961 --> 01:40:55.075
Percy is amazing. It is

01:40:55.076 --> 01:40:58.590
about where QA systems
kind of went awry.

01:40:58.591 --> 01:40:59.920
There is a SQuAD benchmark

